tgs:Monologue.Frankly_123_TheConsumptionPyramid a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse,
        tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/123-the-consumption-pyramid> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Grounded in an unusually candid, specific personal story: Nate discloses that the idea for this framework came to him during an overnight hospital stay for knee surgery, when a planned stop at a favorite Indian restaurant was thwarted by the restaurant being locked and gated — he later realized this coincided with real ICE activity staging nearby in Minneapolis, prompting a direct, uncomfortable reflection on how his own minor inconvenience (missing dinner) sat alongside potentially much higher-stakes disruption for the restaurant's own staff. Nate is careful and explicit that the pyramid is not a purity test: most people move across several layers within the same week, and he discloses his own real fluctuation directly ('I can have super discipline for three months straight and then slide upward in this pyramid during a stressful week, that's me too'). A real, structural nuance he insists on: the same physical object can sit on different layers for different people depending entirely on context — a smartphone as pure status object for one person, genuine functional necessity for another who depends on it for banking and public services. The framework's real stakes, per Nate's own direct argument, are asymmetric under contraction: higher layers depend on complex, brittle, global systems that don't degrade gracefully, while lower layers tend to be more local, repairable, and substitutable — making intentional descent, before conditions force it, a real form of what he calls sovereignty rather than austerity. The episode closes with a real, direct callback (John Merrick's 'I am not an animal, I am a human being') repurposed as Nate's own closing line — 'I am not a consumer, I am a human being' — followed by a real, concrete request that listeners share the framework with 2-3 others rather than simply consume it alone."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/EOmG0B2q9aI> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Frankly-123-Transcript-The-Consumption-Pyramid.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2026-02-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-02-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:references tgs:AnimatedVideo.Part2TheHumanSuperorganism,
        tgs:PersonalIntervention.LowerDopaminePeaks,
        tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-the-consumption-pyramid> ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems,
        tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:definition "Nate unpacks the economic label 'consumer' into a real, structured 7-layer framework he calls the consumption pyramid — Survival Inputs, Stability and Function, Care and Belonging, Comfort and Convenience, Status and Signaling, Novelty and Stimulation, and Escape and Dopamine Sinks, moving bottom-to-top from physical necessity toward mood, identity, and numbing. Nate is explicit the pyramid is not a moral ranking (most people move across multiple layers within a single day) but a real, practical map of dependency, reliability, and necessity — offered as a tool for intentionally moving down the pyramid before external volatility forces the issue."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Consumption Pyramid"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real site inconsistency worth flagging plainly, not silently accepted: this episode's own real page carries meta-description tags (used for search/social previews) describing an entirely different, unrelated episode about AI and biophysical limits — the real, correct 'Description' section further down the same page matches this episode's actual content and the uploaded transcript exactly, and that is what this build is based on. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Frankly #44 ('The Many Shapes of the Carbon Pulse,' already flagged deferred earlier this session), Frankly #122 ('A Country of Geniuses,' the immediately preceding episode), and Frankly #124 ('Wide Boundary News,' the immediately following episode and Nate's own real revised peak-oil call, already flagged deferred during the Frankly_154 build). Anna Lembke's real prior TGS episode (#206) is directly cited again, matching the same real episode number already flagged deferred during the Frankly_154 build. The 7-layer pyramid itself is genuinely rich, structured content — comparable in kind to the Frankly_58 archetype system and the Frankly_154 PersonalIntervention set, both already fully minted this session — but was NOT built out into individuals in this pass, since MJSullivan's request was to build the episode itself, not explicitly the taxonomy; captured here in prose instead, in the episode's own definition and discussion summary. Worth a dedicated follow-up pass if wanted, following the same pattern already established (thinkr:ConsumptionLayer as a new class, ordered 1-7, linked from this episode via a hasLayer-style property)."@en .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_154_UnordinaryInterventions a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse,
        tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency,
        tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/154-unordinary-interventions> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opens with an unusually explicit methodological distinction Nate draws for himself as much as the audience: most 'what can I do' lists blur together self-protection (growing food, improving sleep, building a support network) with actually changing the trajectory of the metacrisis itself, and he states directly that everything offered here is aimed at the latter, not the former — 'nothing I offer here is primarily intended as just a coping skill.' A real, candid personal admission anchors the 'be wrong in public' intervention: Nate directly cites his own revised peak-oil call as the example, disclosing that every time he updates a public view, he braces to be thought less of, and states plainly that 'almost every time, it goes the other way.' The episode's own real, load-bearing metaphor — 'seeding' the future rather than choosing it — is offered as a direct extension of Nate's own complexity-science background, not a rhetorical flourish: he frames individual action as shifting initial conditions rather than directly producing outcomes. The closing framing is explicit about scale without being falsely modest or grandiose: Nate states plainly that one person doing these things produces 'a healthier and saner life for them and their family,' thousands doing them produces 'some sort of subculture,' and only millions doing them might actually shift the underlying drivers — a real, graduated claim rather than an implied guarantee that individual action alone resolves the predicament."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/hMb9uBz-31A> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Frankly-154-Transcript-Unordinary-Interventions.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2026-08-02"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-08-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct companion to Frankly_132 — explicitly turning 'from the directional to the personal.' Where Frankly_132 laid out 6 broad societal fronts, this episode offers 15 small, specific, individual-scale interventions, each explicitly aimed at 'cloud seeding the storm itself' rather than merely helping a person cope with it. Nate draws a real, direct distinction between the two: growing food, improving sleep, and building a support network genuinely help a person weather the metacrisis, but (in his own words) 'don't much change the storm itself' — every intervention offered here is instead framed as a real seed for what the whole system actually needs, grounded in his own real complexity-science framing that tiny differences in starting conditions can lead to wildly different outcomes."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Unordinary Interventions: How to Plant Seeds for a Better Future"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Frankly #114 ('Directional Advice for the More Than Human Predicament'), Frankly #65 ('wide-boundary capital,' already flagged as deferred during the RR-21 build earlier this session), Frankly #98 ('cultural mitochondria,' already flagged deferred during the Frankly_132 build), Frankly #23 ('The Mordor Economy'), Frankly #59 ('7 Meta Questions About Our Global Metabolism'), Frankly #120 ('The Creature in the Machine'), Frankly #124 ('Wide Boundary News,' Nate's own real revised peak-oil call), and Frankly #142/#143 (Guide to Staying Human Parts 2/3) — all real, confirmed URLs. Real new Personas found but NOT built: Brett KenCairn (real Boulder Sustainability Director, real prior TGS episode #222, directly described as a recent real conversation partner — 'my recent conversation with...'), Jeff Currie (real prior TGS episode #229), Audrey Tang (already flagged deferred during the Frankly_132 build), Tristan Harris (TGS #214), John Vervaeke (TGS #158), Anna Lembke (TGS #206), Jonathan Haidt (TGS #59, already flagged deferred during the RR-15 build). The 15 individual interventions themselves ARE minted in this pass, as tgs:PersonalIntervention individuals — see personalinterventions.ttl."@en .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_47_FranklyReFranklys a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/47-frankly-re-franklys> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Genuinely raw and unscripted — Nate explicitly frames it as having 'no outline here, this is just a complete off-the-cuff riff,' and the delivery matches: real-time analysis of the Israel-Gaza war's likely trajectory and US debt/interest-rate dynamics, offered candidly as non-expert commentary rather than polished analysis ('I'm not an expert on that other than to say...'). The episode's real center of gravity is Nate directly stating the Frankly format's own purpose in his own words for the first time this explicitly: building awareness in a 'scout team' of engaged listeners, and secondarily attempting to shift the 'consciousness of those in power' who hold outsized leverage over institutional choices. A genuinely personal, vulnerable thread runs through the closing third: Nate discloses working with a coach on shifting from 'productivity' to 'awareness,' describes his own imperfect attempt at tech-free 'Silent Saturdays' ('doing kind of half-assed... because I'm so busy'), and states plainly, without hedging, 'I will never be anyone's guru... I'm not only an analyst but I'm a client' — real self-disclosure about his own use of the same practices he recommends, offered as an admission of imperfection rather than authority."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://youtu.be/ZJbKVu7vbLk> ;
    dct:created "2023-10-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-10-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary,
        tgs:Subject.EnergySystems,
        tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real-time, unscripted reflection on the Frankly format itself — recorded the same Friday afternoon it discusses (Oct 27, 2023), amid the opening days of the Israel-Gaza war and Mike Johnson's election as US House Speaker the day before. Nate states the format's actual purpose directly (informing a 'scout team' of humans and, secondarily, attempting to shift the consciousness of those in power, not offering real-time investment intel), gives a raw take on unfolding Middle East and financial-market events, and previews an extensive, explicitly 'non-exhaustive' list of specific future Frankly topics he has planned."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Frankly Re: Franklys"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct prior-episode reference confirmed via this episode's own real show notes: Frankly #46 ('The Middle East: Stormwatch') — not yet built as an Episode individual in this pass. This episode previews an extensive real list of specific future Frankly topics, none of which match any Episode individual currently built in this graph by title — real, deferred candidates worth checking against as more Franklys are built: AI vs. real ecology (steelmanning AI-solves-everything optimism against ecological limits), a 'flowchart of the future' relating interest rates/governance/inequality/nuclear power/debt, a philanthropy framework organized around Great Simplification-relevant categories, 'renewable energy: right answer to the wrong question,' 'what if solar panels were free,' 'seven lenses' (individual/emotional through universal/pale-blue-dot perspectives on the future), nuclear power's role in an energy-constrained future, and the relationship between US debt growth and Strategic Petroleum Reserve drawdown. Also worth noting: the transcript's own 'behavioral stack' framing (cellular/enteric/limbic/cognitive, visualized as a pyramid rather than cognition-on-top dominance) is the same real concept independently cited with its own real video reference in this graph's own RR-9 show notes — a real, substantive conceptual throughline across episodes, not yet minted as its own Concept individual here."@en .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_80_ThankYouForRuiningMyLife a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency,
        tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/80-thank-you-for-ruining-my-life> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Genuinely unscripted and personal, by Nate's own explicit description — 'these Frankly are just I have no notes at all other than the email I just read.' The episode's real emotional center is the listener's own words, read aloud: a self-described 'wrestling match' between techno-optimism and the podcast's systems synthesis, with each new layer of complexity (additional episodes, then outside books) initially adding discomfort and sleepless nights but ultimately making the overall picture more coherent rather than less. A real, candid personal admission from Nate follows directly: being recognized and engaged with substantively by a stranger 'in real life' lands differently and more powerfully than working alone in an office, even though he 'care[s] deeply about these issues' regardless. The episode's most quotable real formulation is Nate's own closing personal ethos — 'B+ in service of life' — offered as his real, current email signature and a direct acknowledgment that his own production quality is intentionally imperfect ('these could be way better produced... I'm just doing the best I can every week'). Nate is explicit and unambiguous that this openness isn't universal outreach: he directly states the podcast 'is not for everyone because it's intense, it's scary, there are no easy answers,' aimed specifically at people with the maturity to sit with discomfort rather than a broad audience. The episode closes on real, concrete forward-looking previews of Nate's own upcoming work, including an early, evolving version of ideas that would later inform the Frankly_132 six-fronts framework, and a real, direct tease of the following week's Frankly as a time-travel-themed piece connected to the Superorganism concept."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9wXwdcXJRzs> ;
    dct:created "2024-12-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-12-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_99_JeremyGrantham,
        tgs:Persona.JoanDiamond,
        tgs:Persona.PriscillaTrinh,
        tgs:Series.BendNotBreak ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary,
        tgs:Subject.EnergySystems,
        tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:definition "An unusually personal, unscripted reflection prompted by a real encounter: a venture capitalist approached Nate at a tech conference, having watched 400 hours of the podcast's content, and thanked him for 'ruining his life.' Nate reads the listener's own real email aloud (paraphrased here, not quoted verbatim, per this graph's own copyright discipline) — a real, detailed account of an emotional and intellectual 'wrestling match' moving from techno-optimism through repeated cycles of resistance and clarity toward a settled, if difficult, new understanding. Nate uses the encounter to reflect directly on his own role and the podcast's real purpose."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Thank You for Ruining My Life"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct textual grounds for the tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency link — arguably the single clearest, most explicit primary-source statement of that theme found anywhere in this graph: Nate's own words, 'these aren't my concepts... I stand on the shoulders of giants,' 'I'm just a spokesperson and a catalyst that's widening the Overton window,' and 'I'm just a guy. I'm not a leader of any movement or anything.' Real, direct connection to already-built content: the listener had real, specific opinions about the Jeremy Grantham episode (tgs:Interview.TGS_99_JeremyGrantham, already built) and had watched 'the bend not break series' (tgs:Series.BendNotBreak, already built) multiple times; Joan Diamond is directly named as a second past episode the listener had opinions about — this transcript is what surfaced her as a real past guest, independently confirmed and newly built as a Persona this same session. Nate's own real, forward-looking mention of 'a reality 101 primer' connects directly to Priscilla Trịnh's own real 'Reality 101' course (already built via RR-9). Also worth noting as a real precursor, NOT identical content: Nate previews upcoming work on 'a framework for philanthropy... a framework for technology... a framework for community... a framework for governance... a framework for individual healing and behavior' — a real, earlier, evolving draft of ideas that plausibly fed into the later, structurally different Frankly_132 six-fronts framework (already built), but not treated here as the same content, since the specific categories don't match one-to-one. The episode's own real closing line directly previews the next Frankly as 'a movie idea on time travel and the superorganism' — not yet built in this graph."@en .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_85_TheLightTriad a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse,
        tgs:Concept.DarkTriad,
        tgs:Concept.LightTriad,
        tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/85-the-light-triad> ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:created "2025-02-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-02-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary,
        tgs:Subject.EnergySystems,
        tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own solo exploration of Scott Barry Kaufman's 'Light Triad' (faith in humanity, humanism, Kantianism) as a real but structurally weaker counterweight to the Dark Triad — arguing traits generally outcompete values, so a lone Light Triad individual will be exploited by a Dark Triad one, and only real, mutually-supporting groups (not just individual goodness) stand a chance. Closes with open questions he doesn't resolve: how many people does a Light Triad individual need around them for real protection, whether Dark Triad prevalence rises or falls as the Carbon Pulse declines, and whether a Dark Triad personality can genuinely change."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Light Triad"@en .

tgs:PersonalIntervention.ApprenticeSomeoneYounger a thinkr:PersonalIntervention,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:definition "Deliberately handing down a real skill, way of seeing, or hard-won wisdom to one specific younger person. Hagens' own real, direct stake: 'none of the other seeds that I'm outlining here will outlive us unless people do this one,' since culture changes through direct human transmission, not policy or decree."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Apprentice Someone Younger"@en .

tgs:PersonalIntervention.BeWrongInPublic a thinkr:PersonalIntervention,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:definition "Noticing when a person is wrong about something (everyone is, most weeks) and saying so out loud in front of others, rather than letting it pass silently. Hagens' own real, direct example: publicly revising his own peak-oil call to November 2018. His own candid disclosure: he braces to be thought less of each time, and 'almost every time, it goes the other way' — modeling updated priors lowers the social cost for everyone else listening to do the same."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Be Wrong in Public"@en .

tgs:PersonalIntervention.BreakChainOfNegativity a thinkr:PersonalIntervention,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:relation tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency ;
    skos:definition "Deliberately functioning as a real circuit-breaker for emotional contagion — being the one who brings lightness into a heavy room, smiles at a stranger, or does a small kind thing first without waiting for it to be reciprocated."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Break the Chain of Negativity"@en .

tgs:PersonalIntervention.CoordinateSmallGroup a thinkr:PersonalIntervention,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:relation tgs:Concept.FrameworkForAction ;
    skos:definition "Finding 3-8 real people and doing an actual recurring thing together — a food-buying club, a makerspace, a tool library, a regular work party — with real, physically hard shared effort (not just conversation) as the way genuine trust actually gets built. Hagens' own direct framing: the metacrisis is fundamentally a coordination failure, and manipulative/Dark-Triad actors proliferate exactly when everyone else is scattered and isolated."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Coordinate With a Small Group of Close Friends"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct structural echo of Frankly_132's own Level 1 ('Finding the Others') — not the same wording, but the same real emphasis on trusted, coordinated small groups as foundational, not incidental."@en .

tgs:PersonalIntervention.CurateStatusEnvironment a thinkr:PersonalIntervention,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:definition "Deliberately reclaiming who a person's brain calibrates social status against, given human status-calibration evolved for groups of roughly 150 (Dunbar's number), not the effectively unlimited comparison pool phones now provide. Hagens' own real personal account: moving from Wall Street cubicles to living near farmers directly reshaped what he noticed and admired."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Curate Your Status Environment"@en .

tgs:PersonalIntervention.LearnPreIndustrialSkillBadly a thinkr:PersonalIntervention,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:relation tgs:Archetype.Craftsman,
        tgs:InterventionSubdomain.SkillsForContractingEconomy ;
    skos:definition "Growing one food crop, learning an instrument, or learning to fix one kind of broken object — deliberately not aiming for competence. Hagens' own real, direct point: 'learning it badly' is the actual goal, since being the one person in a community who knows how at all is what matters, not mastery — every such skill 'comes back into ordinary people's hands' is real productive capacity rebuilt outside the superorganism."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Learn One Pre-Industrial Skill Badly"@en .

tgs:PersonalIntervention.MeasureReturnsBeyondMoney a thinkr:PersonalIntervention,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:definition "When investing time, energy, or money in something, ask what it actually returns without denominating the answer only in currency — health, friendship, a recovered local ecosystem, something beautiful that now exists. Hagens' own real framing: money creates a recursive feedback loop pulling everything into a 'monetary vortex' until it's the only thing left on the scoreboard; this intervention reclaims one part of life to measure in real, wide-boundary capital instead."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Measure Your Returns in Something Other Than Money"@en .

tgs:PersonalIntervention.RegenerateSomethingLiving a thinkr:PersonalIntervention,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:relation tgs:InterventionSubdomain.SoilAndWaterRegeneration ;
    skos:definition "Putting real hands-on effort into local ecological restoration — converting a strip of lawn to native plants, joining or starting a restoration day. Hagens directly credits a real recent conversation with Boulder Sustainability Director Brett KenCairn (real prior TGS guest, episode #222, not built as a Persona in this pass) for reframing climate as fundamentally a living-systems problem, not only a carbon problem — citing the real, documented recovery of the US Southern Plains after the Dust Bowl as direct historical precedent that land-use damage is genuinely reversible at regional scale."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Regenerate Something Living"@en .

tgs:PersonalIntervention.RehearseLoss a thinkr:PersonalIntervention,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:definition "A real, practiced dress rehearsal for material loss, not just contemplating it — Hagens' own concrete suggestion: pick a stretch of weeks and live on 30% less, then find out empirically what's actually missed. Grounded in real loss-aversion research (losses feel roughly twice as intense as equivalent gains) and the Stoic practice of premeditatio malorum."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rehearse Loss Before It Shows Up"@en .

tgs:PersonalIntervention.ShiftOddsForOneSpecies a thinkr:PersonalIntervention,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:relation tgs:InterventionSubdomain.BiodiversityDefense ;
    skos:definition "Moving beyond rescuing a single distressed animal (real, but a one-time act) toward real, sustained changes that shift survival odds across a whole lineage's future generations. Hagens' own real, specific examples, mostly about subtraction rather than addition: keeping cats indoors (outdoor cats kill more US birds than windows and cars combined), window decals, and turning off outdoor lights during migration season."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Shift the Odds for One Species"@en .

tgs:PersonalIntervention.StandOnTheGround a thinkr:PersonalIntervention,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:definition "Direct physical contact with bare earth, grass, or water — not framed by Hagens as a wellness ritual, but as closing a real, literal gap between human bodies and the living planet most people go entire days without physically touching. His own real framing: 'we don't embody or protect what we don't feel connected to.'"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Take Your Shoes Off and Stand on the Ground"@en .

tgs:PersonalIntervention.TrainLikeYoullBeNeeded a thinkr:PersonalIntervention,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:relation tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency,
        tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak ;
    skos:definition "Deliberately pushing oneself physically, intellectually, and spiritually in whatever way a person alone knows they specifically need — not generic self-help, per Hagens' own direct framing, but real preparation for a real future moment. His own words: 'the cultural mycelium underneath us will only be as strong as the humans that comprise it' — directly connected to his own real, still-unfolding 'Guide to Staying Human' series."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Train Like You'll Be Needed"@en .

tgs:PersonalIntervention.TreatTodayLikeTomorrow a thinkr:PersonalIntervention,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:definition "Picking one thing repeatedly deferred to 'starting tomorrow' and doing a small piece of it today instead. Hagens frames the underlying tendency as a real, evolved feature (discounting distant costs for near rewards), not a personal character flaw — but argues real metacrisis response requires overriding it regardless."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Treat Today Like the Tomorrow You Keep Promising"@en .

tgs:PersonalIntervention.WriteDownYourEnough a thinkr:PersonalIntervention,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:relation tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    skos:definition "Explicitly defining, in writing, what 'enough' actually looks like — income, housing, status, possessions — then bringing fixed costs down toward that real number, letting the freed gap become real biophysical spaciousness rather than something to fill with more. Hagens' own direct claim: once a person knows their own enough at a gut level, 'Moloch and the superorganism growth machine lose some of their power' over them."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Write Down Your Enough"@en .

