    skos:prefLabel "Rachel Carson"@en .

tgs:Human.ReidMeloy a thinkr:Human,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:comment "Forensic psychologist. Former clinical professor of psychiatry, UC San Diego School of Medicine. FBI Behavioral Analysis Unit consultant since 2002. Author/editor of 11 books including 'The Psychopathic Mind' and 'International Handbook of Threat Assessment' (with Jens Hoffmann); over 220 peer-reviewed publications."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <http://dbpedia.org/resource/J._Reid_Meloy> ;
    skos:prefLabel "J. Reid Meloy"@en .

tgs:Human.RexWeyler a thinkr:Human,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:comment "American-Canadian writer, journalist, and ecologist (b. 1947). Director of the original Greenpeace Foundation (1973-1982), campaign photographer, editor of the Greenpeace Chronicles, and co-founder of Greenpeace International (1979). Author of 'Blood of the Land' (Pulitzer Prize-nominated Native American history) and 'Greenpeace: The Inside Story.' Writes the 'Deep Green' column at Greenpeace International's own site since 2008."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <http://dbpedia.org/resource/Rex_Weyler> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rex Weyler"@en .

tgs:Human.RichardGephardt a thinkr:Human,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:comment "American attorney, lobbyist, and politician (b. January 31, 1941, St. Louis, Missouri); Democratic member, US House of Representatives, Missouri's 3rd district (1977-2005) — 28 years in Congress. House Majority Leader (1989-1995); House Minority Leader (1995-2003). Twice sought the Democratic presidential nomination (1988, 2004). President and CEO, Gephardt Group (later Gephardt Government Affairs), founded 2005. Real early career: precinct captain (1968), Alderman for St. Louis's 14th Ward (1971-1976). BS, Northwestern University (1962); JD, University of Michigan Law School (1965). Author of 'An Even Better Place' and 'The American Immigrant: The Outsiders,' among others. Board service includes Ford Motor Company, US Steel Corporation, Spirit AeroSystems, Centene Corporation, and Chair, Board of Trustees, Scripps Research Institute; founder, Gephardt Institute for Civic and Community Engagement at Washington University in St. Louis."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dick_Gephardt> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Richard Gephardt"@en .

tgs:Human.RichardSchwartz a thinkr:Human,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:comment "American psychotherapist and creator of Internal Family Systems (IFS), developed in the early 1980s out of his own work as a family therapist. Founder, IFS Institute (originally Center for Self Leadership, 2000). Teaching Associate in Psychiatry, Cambridge Health Alliance (Harvard Medical School teaching affiliate). Author/co-author of 10+ books including 'No Bad Parts' (2021) and 'Internal Family Systems Therapy' (1997). Lives near Chicago with his wife Jeanne (Vice President, IFS Institute), three daughters, and six grandchildren."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <http://dbpedia.org/resource/Richard_C._Schwartz> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Richard C. Schwartz"@en .

