tgs:Interview.TGS_1_RichardGephardt a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/1-richard-gephardt> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely personal episode from its opening minutes — Nate's first real question isn't about policy but about why an 80-year-old with a storied career isn't simply retired, and Gephardt's own answer is a real, extended account of his working-class St. Louis childhood: a father whose back injury ended his trucking career, a mother who went to work, and a real, specific formative moment — a high school speech teacher personally arranging a Northwestern summer scholarship that Gephardt directly credits as opening his entire adult path. The episode's real analytical core is Gephardt's own direct comparison between the 9/11 aftermath and January 6th: he offers a real, personal account of running through a small Missouri town on the Christmas after 9/11 and finding every house flying an American flag in solidarity with New York, offered as concrete evidence of genuine national unity — directly contrasted with January 6th, which he attributes to Americans who had been, in his own words, effectively 'brainwashed' by an alternative-reality information ecosystem into believing violence was justified. Gephardt is careful throughout to extend real empathy alongside his critique, stating directly that he understands the mindset that produced January 6th even while condemning the outcome. The episode's closing stretch turns genuinely personal and practical: Gephardt's own real daily routine (two meals within an eight-hour window, a standing bowl of raw vegetables) is directly credited by Nate as something he adopted into his own life as a result of this conversation. The episode closes with real, warm testimony to Gephardt's own reputation — Nate directly relays that mutual colleagues refer to him, half-joking, as 'a saint and a national treasure and a superman' — and Gephardt's own closing words return to a real, concrete personal ethic borrowed from his time as a Boy Scout: leave the campsite better than you found it."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.RichardGephardt ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:created "2021-12-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary,
        tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:definition "The show's real first-ever episode. Nate hosts former US House Majority/Minority Leader Richard Gephardt — a real, direct professional collaborator on a project called 'advance policy,' not just a first-time guest — for Gephardt's own real first-ever podcast appearance of any kind. Covers the real conflict between social-media business models and functioning democracy, a direct real contrast between the national unity following 9/11 and the division surrounding January 6th, and Gephardt's own real, extensive account of his path from a working-class St. Louis upbringing into public service."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Richard Gephardt: Democracy, Social Media, and the Case for Public Service"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct date inconsistency in the source material, flagged honestly rather than silently resolved: the show notes list 'Episode 1... Dec 9, 2019' as the issued date, but 'Recorded on: Dec 17, 2021' — an issued date nearly 2 years BEFORE the stated recording date, which is not just unusual but chronologically impossible. This is almost certainly a real site/metadata error, not a genuine fact — further supported by comparison: tgs:Interview.TGS_15_DanielPauly (Episode 15, already built) issued April 2022, only ~2 months after its own Feb 2022 recording — a real, consistent pattern this episode's stated 2019 issue date would badly violate for only 14 episodes' difference. dct:issued deliberately left unasserted here rather than guessing between two implausible options; dct:created (the real recording date) is asserted with confidence. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Tristan Harris and Daniel Schmachtenberger's real joint appearance on the Joe Rogan podcast, directly cited (Schmachtenberger already a real, built Persona in this graph); Tom Downey, directly named as 'a mutual colleague' of Nate and Gephardt, real but not independently confirmed or built in this pass."@en .

tgs:Interview.TGS_213_ChristineWebb a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:SchoolOfThought.TechnoOptimism ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/213-christine-webb> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Webb is careful from the outset to frame human exceptionalism as a specific cultural worldview rather than a species-wide trait — direct, real evidence she offers: it is not correlated with any human universal, and even the book's own title risks implying otherwise. A real, substantive methodological critique threads through the conversation: Webb argues mainstream scientific practice itself often unintentionally reinforces human exceptionalism, and directly cites the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle as an analogy for how the very act of studying animals in artificial lab conditions can distort what's actually being measured. A real, pointed cross-cultural comparison anchors the conversation's political dimension: Webb and Nate discuss the Netherlands' real political parties explicitly organized around animal interests (Party for the Animals, Peace for Animals) as a live counterexample to the US's own more anthropocentric default, while Webb is careful not to romanticize this, directly noting real, current Dutch immigration tensions in the same breath. The episode's most ethically pointed claim is Webb's own direct assertion of a real causal and correlational link between believing in a strict human-animal divide and holding prejudiced attitudes toward human outgroups — she extends this historically to the real dehumanization of women in ancient Greece and of enslaved people. A real, practical closing recommendation structures much of the interview's final third: Webb's own 'slow looking' exercise (sustained, unstructured attention to another living being — a bird feeder, a rock formation, a pigeon) which she assigns to her own students and reports produces a real, noticeable shift in perception within about a week. The episode closes with real, candid personal disclosure from Nate about his own recent two-month recording break and total knee replacement surgery, and Webb's own closing 'magic wand' answer is offered without hedging: a pill that would induce more humility, awe, and wonder toward the rest of nature."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ChristineWebb ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/ArHrLzkJm_k> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/TGS-213-Christine-Webb-Transcript.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2026-02-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:references tgs:InterventionSubdomain.LongTermMandateInstitutions,
        tgs:Persona.DanielPauly,
        tgs:Persona.PriscillaTrinh,
        tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts primatologist Christine Webb, author of 'The Arrogant Ape,' to examine human exceptionalism — the belief that humans are separate from and superior to the rest of nature — as a real, specific cultural ideology (not a human universal, and not primarily biological) that Webb argues sits at the root of the ecological crisis. Drawing on her own real primate research, she walks through traits once assumed uniquely human (tool use, language, culture, theory of mind, art, self-awareness) that are documented across other species, and argues for reimagining economic, legal, and educational systems around the intrinsic value of all life rather than human exclusivity."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Questioning Human Exceptionalism: How Rethinking Our Place in the Web of Life Could Change Our Global Crises"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct cross-reference confirmed: the Netherlands' real child-mayor practice, cited here, is the same real example already used in tgs:InterventionSubdomain.LongTermMandateInstitutions (built during the Frankly_132 six-fronts work) — independent convergence, not a designed link. Daniel Pauly's own real solo TGS episode (#15, 'shifting baselines'), cited in this episode's own show notes, is now built (tgs:Interview.TGS_15_DanielPauly) — resolving what this note previously flagged as a gap. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Jonathan Haidt (TGS #59, already flagged deferred multiple times this session — the fourth independent citation), Jennifer Jacquet, Robin Wall Kimmerer, Karline Janmaat, Jane Goodall, Dian Fossey, and Barbara McClintock, all real, directly cited, none built."@en .

