        tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:definition "Opens the series by naming a pattern Hagens says he hears repeatedly from the audience: people who have already settled on one storyline about the future and filter everything else through it. Introduces Scenario Thinking as the alternative, names 3 reasons holding multiple futures at once is hard in practice (the nervous system wants resolution, careers/identities attach to particular futures, cultural incentives reward confident stories over honest uncertainty), and closes by introducing Shortfall Risk, which the episode's own description states will act as connective tissue across the rest of the series."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "How to Think About the Future (Part 1): Changing the Future Starts with How You Think"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "REBUILT 2026-08-13 from Frankly_138's own official show-notes page (thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/138-...) — a real, structured, WordPress-generated per-episode topic index Hagens' team maintains, discovered this session as a much stronger extraction source than transcript-reading or generic NER. Every thinkr:discusses/dct:references link above traces to a specific labeled entry in that page, not inferred. Fixed 2 real bugs from this stub's first draft: thinkr:hasCanonicalSource was used twice (its own scopeNote documents cardinality-one; the essay link now correctly uses the general dct:source instead) and was typed as an xsd:anyURI string literal rather than a proper linked resource (thinkr:hasCanonicalSource is declared owl:ObjectProperty, not a datatype property — every other use of it in this graph uses a bare IRI)."@en .

