        tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheGreatSimplification,
        tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheStewardshipDeal,
        tgs:SchoolOfThought.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/139-how-to-think-about-the-future-part-2> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely foundational episode for this graph's own scenario-matrix structure, not just another Frankly. Nate opens by directly naming a cognitive trap — settling on one preferred future and then judging everything else against it — and argues for holding a real distribution of possible futures instead. The four-grid structure is built up methodically and explicitly incompletely: Nate states outright that technology and demographics were both considered for their own separate grids and deliberately excluded, arguing both function as amplifiers of whatever direction the underlying system is already moving rather than independent axes — AI specifically is framed as already inside the Earth Systems grid, since data-center electricity demand is itself a direct new draw on the energy/biophysical system the whole framework is built around. A real, distinctive moment of self-aware epistemic humility: Nate explicitly invites the audience to propose grids he hasn't considered, naming mental/physical health and freedom of information/communication as real candidates he chose not to include. The Geopolitical grid's own real-time grounding is notable — recorded during the actual 2026 Iran war and Strait of Hormuz closure, Nate uses that live, unfolding event as a direct real-world example of the 'adversarial and interdependent' quadrant, citing the Philippines' energy emergency, Bangladesh's school closures, and Japan's emergency oil-reserve release as concurrent real evidence. Nate draws a real, pointed distinction between what a contraction-vs-growth headline means for the 'fairness' question (during growth) versus the 'is there enough' question (during contraction) — the same quadrant axes, he argues, describe a fundamentally different lived experience depending on which economic direction they're paired with. The episode closes by explicitly refusing to let any single quadrant stand as a full future on its own — Nate states directly that nobody will 'live in The Great Simplification in general,' only in a Great Simplification combined with a specific power structure, geopolitical backdrop, and ecological condition, previewing Part 3's work building concrete scenario composites from these four layers."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/5R0oUGPf9C4> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Frankly-139-Transcript-How-to-Think-About-the-Future-Part-2.pdf> ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 2 ;
    dct:created "2026-04-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.HowToThinkAboutTheFuture ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_12_DennisMeadows,
        tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger,
        tgs:Series.BendNotBreak,
        tgs:Work.ScenarioThinkingHistoricalEvolution ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-how-to-think-about-the-future-92e> ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior,
        tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:definition "2ND PASS 2026-08-13, using the full real transcript (previously built from show notes only). This is the episode that formally introduces the complete 4-Dimension/16-Facet scenario framework this graph's own ScenarioDimension/ScenarioFacet structure is built from — Nate treats his long-standing 4-scenario Economic grid (Green Growth, Mordor, The Great Simplification, Mad Max) as the foundation, then layers three more grids on top: Power and Distribution (concentration of decision-making authority vs. where material gains flow), Geopolitical (cooperation vs. adversarial relations, crossed with interdependence vs. self-sufficiency), and Earth Systems (climate stress vs. biosphere integrity, explicitly from an already-compromised 2026 baseline, not a neutral one). Technology and demographics are both explicitly considered and deliberately NOT given their own grids — Nate argues both act as modifiers/amplifiers of whatever direction the underlying system is already moving, rather than independent dimensions."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "How to Think About the Future (Part 2): Four Variables Shaping the Coming Decades"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "2ND PASS 2026-08-13 — MJSullivan flagged this as 'the key episode that describes these quadrants' given the axis-correction work done this session (see ScenarioDimension.Economic and ScenarioDimension.Geopolitical's own scopeNotes), and supplied the full transcript for a proper enrichment pass rather than relying on show notes alone. discusses list corrected from the original single tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification (now split into Concept + ScenarioFacet — see that split's own history) to the specific tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheGreatSimplification, and substantially expanded: this episode is the real primary source for all 16 ScenarioFacets across all 4 Dimensions, not just the original 4 Economic ones it had been scoped to before this pass. tgs:Concept.Superorganism added as a real, direct reference — Hagens' own words describing the Cold War Facet as 'the fission of the Superorganism.' Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: 'Fire apes' (a real recurring Reality Blind term, cited directly to a specific page); the real statistic that 26 people hold the same financial claims on biophysical reality as the bottom 4 billion humans (sourced to a real Oxfam report); Concept.DarkTriad already built elsewhere in this graph is referenced again here but not formally dct:referenced in this pass, to avoid overloading a single episode's reference list without a clear stopping point. hasCanonicalSource fixed 2026-08-13 (original 1st-pass note, still accurate) — same xsd:anyURI-literal-instead-of-proper-resource bug originally found and fixed on Frankly_138, never applied to the rest of this series until now."@en .

