    skos:prefLabel "CalDEC — The California Doughnut Snapshot and Report (2025)"@en .

tgs:Work.Belly a thinkr:Work,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:source <https://www.triarchypress.net/belly.html> ;
    rdfs:comment "Author: Nora Bateson. Published September 1, 2026 by Triarchy Press. Described by its own publisher as 'a cookbook for life' — an exploration of nth-order effects and 'the among-ing of all life' through food, family, illness, migration, and ecology."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Belly"@en .

tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury a thinkr:Work,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.DJWhite,
        tgs:Human.NateHagens ;
    dct:source <https://read.realityblind.world/view/388478403/i/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Co-authored by Nate Hagens and DJ White — the second of their real, confirmed co-authored books (see tgs:Work.RealityBlind for the first; a third remains unidentified despite Nate's own direct reference to 'three books' in the RR-24 transcript). Hosted on the same reader platform as Reality Blind, under a different work ID. Cited directly in this graph via real show-notes links for at least 3 distinct concepts across built episodes: the Maximum Power Principle, Peak Oil, and Energetic Remoteness — functioning as a real, load-bearing source text in this graph's own citation network, not just a title mention."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Bottlenecks of the 21st Century"@en .

tgs:Work.Combining a thinkr:Work,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:source <https://www.triarchypress.net/combining.html> ;
    rdfs:comment "Author: Nora Bateson. Published September 2023 by Triarchy Press, 386pp. A poetic, relational exploration of the ecology of communication and interdependency — introduces/elaborates 'Aphanipoiesis' (the unseen ways life comes together) and connects to her earlier-coined 'Warm Data.'"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Combining"@en .

tgs:Work.ComplexityAndPostmodernism a thinkr:Work,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:comment "1998 book by Paul Cilliers (Routledge), cited in Frankly_138's show notes alongside the episode's complex-systems-thinking discussion. Author/title NOT independently re-verified this session — taken directly from the official show notes' own citation, which is itself a stronger source than an unverified transcript mention."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Complexity and Postmodernism: Understanding Complex Systems"@en .

tgs:Work.FutureCouncilFilm a thinkr:Work,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.DamonGameau ;
    dct:issued "2024"^^xsd:gYear ;
    rdfs:comment "2024 documentary film directed by Damon Gameau, following eight children (ages 11-14) across Europe by biodiesel-fueled bus, confronting corporate leaders (Nestlé, Decathlon, ING, among others) about climate accountability. Features an animated antagonist character, 'Groth,' explicitly based on Nate Hagens' own real 'Economic Superorganism' framing (tgs:Concept.Superorganism) — a direct, real instance of this graph's own foundational concept being adapted into mainstream cultural media. Premiered Melbourne International Film Festival (Aug 2024); later screened at the UN General Assembly; theatrical release planned 2025 across Australia, Europe, and America."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Future Council"@en .

tgs:Work.GlobalTippingPointsReport2025 a thinkr:Work,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:comment "Cited in Frankly_138's show notes specifically for its treatment of ice-sheet and soil-system tipping points. Full authorship/publisher NOT independently verified this session — real, citable report per the official show notes' own link, worth a proper WebFetch verification pass before treating this stub as complete."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Global Tipping Points Report 2025"@en .

tgs:Work.ScenarioThinkingHistoricalEvolution a thinkr:Work,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:creator "Peter McKiernan"@en,
        "R. Bradley MacKay"@en ;
    dct:issued "2018"^^xsd:gYear ;
    rdfs:comment "2018 academic work (Cambridge University Press, 'Elements in Business Strategy' series) by R. Bradley MacKay (University of St Andrews) and Peter McKiernan (University of Strathclyde). Traces scenario thinking's roots to ancient civilizations (including Mesopotamian and Assyrian foresight practices) through the French and Anglo-American schools of modern scenario planning, arguing scenario thinking should be understood as distinct from and more fundamental than scenario planning as a managerial technique."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108571494> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Scenario Thinking: A Historical Evolution of Strategic Foresight"@en .

tgs:Work.TaoOfPhysics a thinkr:Work,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.FritjofCapra ;
    dct:issued "1975"^^xsd:gYear ;
    rdfs:comment "1975 book by Fritjof Capra, his real first and best-known work — still in print in 40+ editions, referenced alongside a statue of Shiva in the courtyard of CERN. Argues for real parallels between quantum theory/relativity and Eastern mystical traditions (Hinduism, Buddhism, Taoism), directly inspired by his own real undergraduate reading of Werner Heisenberg's 'Physics and Philosophy.'"@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.fritjofcapra.net/about/> ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Tao of Physics: An Exploration of the Parallels Between Modern Physics and Eastern Mysticism"@en .

tgs:Work.TheMasterAndHisEmissary a thinkr:Work,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.IainMcGilchrist ;
    rdfs:comment "2009 book by Iain McGilchrist (Yale University Press). Argues the brain's two hemispheres offer fundamentally different kinds of attention to the world, and that Western culture has become dominated by narrow, left-hemisphere thinking at the expense of the right's broader, contextual awareness. Sold over 200,000 copies."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <http://dbpedia.org/resource/The_Master_and_His_Emissary> ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Master and His Emissary: The Divided Brain and the Making of the Western World"@en .

tgs:Work.TheMatterWithThings a thinkr:Work,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.IainMcGilchrist ;
    rdfs:comment "2021 book by Iain McGilchrist (Perspectiva Press), extending The Master and His Emissary's hemisphere hypothesis into epistemology and metaphysics — 1,500 pages across two volumes."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <http://dbpedia.org/resource/The_Matter_with_Things> ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Matter with Things: Our Brains, Our Delusions, and the Unmaking of the World"@en .

tgs:Work.ThinkingLikeAMountain a thinkr:Work,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JohnSeed ;
    dct:issued "1988"^^xsd:gYear ;
    rdfs:comment "1988 book (New Society Publishers), co-authored by John Seed, Joanna Macy, Arne Naess, and Pat Fleming — describes the Council of All Beings ritual and the philosophical/experiential foundations of deep ecology. Translated into 12+ languages. Directly discussed in RR-17 as the founding text of the Work That Reconnects lineage."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Thinking Like a Mountain: Towards a Council of All Beings"@en .

tgs:Work.WildEdgeOfSorrow a thinkr:Work,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.FrancisWeller ;
    rdfs:comment "Bestselling book by Francis Weller (North Atlantic Books), the real, direct source of the 'Five Gates of Grief' framework discussed at length in TGS_167. Real endorsement from Anderson Cooper ('One of the best books on grief I have ever read') and from Joanna Macy, already discussed extensively in this graph's own RR-17 build."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.francisweller.net/books.html> ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Wild Edge of Sorrow: Rituals of Renewal and the Sacred Work of Grief"@en .

thinkr: a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:comment "TGS-core: reusable ontology (classes, properties, supporting-class instances) shared across any thinker's knowledge graph built on this pattern."@en .

thinkr:PersonEntityType.Contributor a thinkr:PersonEntityType,
