tgs:AlternateTermType.Abbreviation a thinkr:AlternateTermType,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Abbreviation"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "ADDED 2026-08-13, raised by MJSullivan. The second type NOT reused from SKOS (alongside Antonym) — SKOS's thesaurus relations describe semantic distance, not lexical form, and have no native concept of 'shortened spelling of the same term.' Answers a different question than the other 6 types (lexical form vs. semantic closeness) — see this class's own rdfs:comment for the full reasoning. Real pilot case: tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment's own former compound prefLabel ('EROI (Energy Return on Investment)') split using this exact pattern, same as tgs:Concept.Wetware was the original pilot for the whole mechanism. STANDING CONVENTION, added same day per MJSullivan's explicit direction: when a term IS an Abbreviation of another, the FULL EXPANDED FORM is canonical — it becomes the parent Concept's own skos:prefLabel AND IRI local name. The abbreviation itself goes into the AlternateTerm, never the reverse. This anticipates the NER-extraction pipeline surfacing many more abbreviations at volume — a consistent canonical-form rule now avoids re-litigating this per case later. Applies going forward to every new Abbreviation-typed AlternateTerm, and was applied retroactively to the EnergyReturnOnInvestment pilot itself (its first draft had this backwards)."@en .

tgs:AnimatedVideo.Part2TheHumanSuperorganism a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse,
        tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oNewKEOby80> ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.AnimatedVideo ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oNewKEOby80> ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 2 ;
    dct:contributor "Caleb Bond (Illustrator)"@en ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries ;
    skos:definition "Real, direct explanation of the Carbon Pulse and Superorganism concepts as narration — energy as 'the currency of life,' the real biophysical relationship between money, technology, and finite energy/materials ('money is ultimately a direct claim on energy and resources'), and how growth-oriented institutions have self-organized around fossil-fuel-derived surplus, closing with the real, direct line: 'the human species... has become a mindless, insatiable, energy hungry, super organism.'"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Human Superorganism (Part 2 of 4)"@en .

tgs:Concept.LightTriad a thinkr:Concept,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:contrastsWith tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.ScottBarryKaufman ;
    dct:source <https://doaj.org/article/6a8567c65a0a46cbb579e2a93b07acd0> ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:definition "Scott Barry Kaufman's proposed counterpart to the Dark Triad (2019, with David Bryce Yaden, Elizabeth Hyde, and Eli Tsukayama) — a cluster of three prosocial traits: Kantianism (treating people as ends in themselves, not means), humanism (belief in the inherent dignity of others), and faith in humanity (a basic trust that most people are good). Hagens' own explicit framing treats it as a real but structurally weaker force than the Dark Triad — a value-based orientation up against trait-based behavior, requiring deliberate group-level support (not just individual goodness) to hold its own."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Light Triad"@en .

tgs:Concept.MoneyAsClaimOnEnergy a thinkr:Concept,
        thinkr:HagensConcept,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:echoesIdeaOf tgs:Persona.Aristotle ;
    dct:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com> ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.MonetarySystemAndGrowth ;
    skos:definition "Hagens' framing of money not as wealth itself but as a social/legal claim on future energy and resources — meaning monetary growth (via debt, interest) implicitly presumes continued growth in real energy throughput, tying the Growth Imperative concept directly to biophysical limits rather than treating them as separate domains."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Money as a Claim on Future Energy"@en .

tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament a thinkr:Concept,
        thinkr:HagensConcept,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com> ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:prefLabel "More-Than-Human Predicament"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "GENUINE OPEN QUESTION, not resolved as of 2026-08-13: may be the SAME underlying idea as the existing tgs:Concept.HumanPredicament, just reframed with explicitly non-anthropocentric ('more-than-human') language — or may be a real, deliberate broadening Hagens is making, extending the predicament framing to include non-human life rather than only humanity's own situation. Deliberately stubbed SEPARATELY rather than silently merged into HumanPredicament or silently treated as a duplicate — a later pass with more source material (this stub currently has only its show-notes timestamp label, no elaborated definition) should resolve which reading is correct, and either merge the two or keep them genuinely distinct with a real thinkr:relatesTo link explaining why."@en .

tgs:Concept.Symmathesy a thinkr:Concept,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NoraBateson ;
    dct:source <https://journals.isss.org/index.php/proceedings59th/article/view/2720> ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:definition "Nora Bateson's 2015 coinage (Greek 'sym' + 'mathesis,' together + learning) for an entity composed through contextual mutual learning by interaction — a living system (a family, an ecosystem, a conversation) that is genuinely shaped by its own internal relationships and interdependencies, not just a collection of independently-acting parts."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Symmathesy"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "dcterms:source UPGRADED 2026-08-13 from a generic WordPress blog post to the real ISSS (International Society for the Systems Sciences) academic conference proceedings — a strictly more authoritative citation for this same 2015 coinage, surfaced via RR-26's own real linked show notes."@en .

tgs:Concept.Wetware a thinkr:Concept,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:echoesIdeaOf tgs:Persona.SigmundFreud ;
    thinkr:hasAlternateTerm [ a thinkr:AlternateTerm ;
            thinkr:hasAlternateTermType tgs:AlternateTermType.ExactMatch ;
            skos:prefLabel "Evolutionary Mismatch"@en ;
            skos:scopeNote "PILOT CASE for the alternate-term mechanism, 2026-08-13 — split out of this Concept's own former compound prefLabel (\"Human 'Wetware' / Evolutionary Mismatch\"), which had jammed two real terms into one string. Kept as an anonymous blank node (Tier 2, per MJSullivan's own hasAlternateTerm pattern) rather than promoted to a named thinkr:AlternateTerm individual — not yet reused across any other Concept, no independent citable history captured yet. Promote to a named individual if/when either changes." ] ;
    dct:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wetware_(brain)> ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.BehavioralEconomicsAndCognition ;
    skos:definition "The idea that human cognitive and emotional wiring evolved for small-group, resource-scarce Pleistocene environments and is poorly matched to modern abundance, abstraction, and global-scale decision-making — a recurring lens Hagens uses to explain collectively self-destructive behavior."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Human 'Wetware'"@en .

tgs:Human.ChristineWebb a thinkr:Human,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:comment "American primatologist; Assistant Professor, Department of Environmental Studies (Animal Studies program), New York University. Previously Researcher and Lecturer, Department of Human Evolutionary Biology, Harvard University. Research spans two intersecting lines: social dynamics in non-human primates, and how the dominant narrative of human exceptionalism has shaped scientific knowledge of the more-than-human world. Author of 'The Arrogant Ape: The Myth of Human Exceptionalism and Why It Matters' (2025, Penguin Random House) — a real New York Times Notable Book of 2025, longlisted for the Nayef Al-Rodhan Book Prize."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.cewebb.com/about> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Christine Webb"@en .

tgs:Human.DamonGameau a thinkr:Human,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:comment "Australian actor, director, and producer (b. 1982, Adelaide); trained at NIDA. Directed 'That Sugar Film' (2015, highest-grossing Australian cinema documentary at the time) and '2040' (2019, Berlin Film Festival premiere; framed as a letter to his young daughter about achievable climate solutions; also authored the companion book '2040: A Handbook for the Regeneration'). Co-founder, Regen Studios (with Anna Kaplan), building impact campaigns for his films across classrooms, boardrooms, and parliaments. Named Australian of the Year finalist (2020) for his Regeneration-related advocacy work. Director of 'Future Council' (2024), which follows eight children ages 11-14 confronting corporate leaders (including Nestlé, Decathlon, and ING) about climate accountability; premiered at Melbourne International Film Festival (Aug 2024) and later screened at the UN General Assembly."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Damon_Gameau> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Damon Gameau"@en .

tgs:Human.DanielChristianWahl a thinkr:Human,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:comment "German-born sustainability consultant, educator, and whole-systems designer; catalyst of the 'rising regeneration' movement. BSc Biology (University of Edinburgh, 1996; also studied UC Santa Cruz, 1995), MSc Holistic Science (Schumacher College, 2001), PhD Design for Human and Planetary Health (University of Dundee, 2006). Former Director, Findhorn College (2007-2010). Author of 'Designing Regenerative Cultures' (2016, Triarchy Press; translated into 7+ languages). Winner, 2021 RSA Bicentenary Medal; 2022 Volans Fellow. Lives on Mallorca, Spain."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Daniel Christian Wahl"@en .

tgs:Human.FrancisWeller a thinkr:Human,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:comment "American psychotherapist (MFT, retired), writer, and self-described soul activist; 40+ years developing what he calls soul-centered psychotherapy, synthesizing psychology, anthropology, mythology, alchemy, indigenous cultures, and poetic traditions. Founder and director, WisdomBridge (educational programs integrating indigenous and Western wisdom traditions). Author of the bestselling 'The Wild Edge of Sorrow: Rituals of Renewal and the Sacred Work of Grief' (real, direct originator of the 'Five Gates of Grief' framework), 'The Threshold Between Loss and Revelation' (with Rashani Réa), 'In the Absence of the Ordinary: Soul Work for Times of Uncertainty,' and 'Entering the Healing Ground' (the official Wild Edge of Sorrow workbook). Real endorsement from Joanna Macy (already discussed extensively in this graph's own RR-17 build) appears on his own book jacket. BA, University of Wisconsin-Green Bay; two Master's degrees, John F. Kennedy University."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.francisweller.net/> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Francis Weller"@en .

tgs:Human.FritjofCapra a thinkr:Human,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:comment "Austrian-born American physicist, systems theorist, and deep ecologist (b. February 1, 1939, Vienna). PhD, theoretical physics, University of Vienna (1966; doctoral advisor Walter Thirring); 20 years in particle-physics research at institutions including University of Paris, Lawrence Berkeley Laboratory, UC Santa Cruz, UC Berkeley, and San Francisco State University. Founding director (1995-2020), Center for Ecoliteracy, Berkeley; faculty, Amana-Key executive education program (São Paulo, Brazil); Fellow, Schumacher College (UK). Author of several real international bestsellers, including 'The Tao of Physics' (1975, still in print in 40+ editions), 'The Turning Point' (1982), 'The Web of Life' (1996), and 'The Hidden Connections' (2002); co-author with Pier Luigi Luisi of 'The Systems View of Life: A Unifying Vision' (Cambridge University Press, 2014)."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fritjof_Capra> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fritjof Capra"@en .

tgs:Human.JohnSeed a thinkr:Human,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:comment "Australian environmental activist, musician, and deep ecology facilitator; founder/director, Rainforest Information Centre (1979-present), campaigning to save New South Wales's sub-tropical rainforests — direct actions in 1979 (200 arrests) led to protection of what became Nightcap National Park, later a UNESCO World Heritage site. Co-creator, with Joanna Macy, of the Council of All Beings ritual (1985) and, more broadly, the experiential deep-ecology processes underlying the Work That Reconnects. Co-author, 'Thinking Like a Mountain: Towards a Council of All Beings' (1988, with Joanna Macy, Arne Naess, and Pat Fleming; translated into 12+ languages). Order of Australia Medal recipient (1995) for services to conservation and the environment."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Seed> ;
    skos:prefLabel "John Seed"@en .

tgs:Human.SamanthaPower a thinkr:Human,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:comment "American regenerative economist, futurist, and bioregionalist, based in Oakland, CA; co-founder/director, the BioFi Project; founder, Finance for Gaia. Former World Bank nature finance specialist, advising finance ministries, central banks, and investors. Co-author (with Leon Seefeld), 'Bioregional Financing Facilities: Reimagining Finance to Regenerate Our Planet' (2024) — a collaboration between Finance for Gaia, Dark Matter Labs Capital Systems, and the Buckminster Fuller Institute Design Lab, with advisors including tgs:Persona.MatthewMonahan (already a real, built Persona in this graph, though not mentioned directly in this episode's own transcript)."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Samantha Power"@en .

