tgs:Interview.TGS_126_AVisionForBetterment a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse,
        tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/126-daniel-schmachtenberger-7> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opens with real, direct continuity from a prior conversation — Nate frames this explicitly as 'kind of the first followup' to an earlier discussion on AI and the superorganism roughly a year prior. Schmachtenberger's real core distinction, stated with unusual precision: naive progress achieves a narrowly-defined goal while externalizing harm elsewhere, then generates a self-congratulatory narrative from survivorship bias — told by 'those that happen to make it through,' not the losers of wars, the extinct species, or the colonized. He is careful to characterize this generously as naive, and less generously as 'propaganda and apologism for winners.' A real, extended historical section traces this pattern through named figures (Genghis Khan credited with roughly 40 million deaths, 11% of the world's population at the time) directly alongside real counter-examples of pre-modern constraint-design systems — the Iroquois Confederacy and Pacific Northwest tribal fishing rules cited as real, working precedents for building limits into a system's own structure rather than relying on individual restraint. The conversation's most concrete, quantified section covers PFAS contamination directly: a real cited $10.3 billion 3M settlement, a real single-state remediation cost estimate of $50 million, and a real, striking claim that removing all PFAS at current production rates would cost roughly $200 trillion annually — about twice global GDP. A real, pointed institutional critique follows: the 14th Amendment's corporate-personhood application, with a real, specific historical claim that by 1912 the Supreme Court had heard 28 cases on the rights of African Americans against 312 on the rights of corporations under the same amendment. Schmachtenberger's own closing framing is genuinely reflective rather than prescriptive: he proposes 'yellow teaming' — tracing an action's full network of causal effects before building it, using a healthy tree's cascading positive externalities (to soil, water, insects, other species) as the real model for what well-designed action looks like — and closes by explicitly cautioning against rushing into action, advocating instead for 'a deeper movement into withdrawal' and honest reckoning with how much of the impulse to act quickly is itself unprocessed ego or avoidance. Nate's own closing response is genuinely personal and unscripted: rather than summarizing, he says he's going to go sit by a tree at dusk and reflect."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tmusbHBKW84> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/TGS126DanielSchmachtenbergerTranscript.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2024-05-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-06-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:references tgs:Human.VanessaAndreotti,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_99_JeremyGrantham,
        tgs:Persona.IainMcGilchrist ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary,
        tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:definition "FULLY BUILT 2026-08-13 from a stub, using the full real transcript (over 3 hours, 3h21m real runtime). Nate welcomes back Daniel Schmachtenberger to unpack a real, multi-authored Consilience Project paper, 'Development in Progress,' distinguishing naive/immature progress (achieving narrowly-defined goals while externalizing harm, then telling a survivorship-biased story about it) from authentic progress. Ranges across evolutionary history, the maximum power principle, historical strongmen (Genghis Khan, Attila, Alexander), indigenous constraint-design systems (Iroquois Confederacy, Pacific Northwest fishing rules), corporate personhood's real 14th Amendment origins, and real, specific PFAS/environmental-cost data, before closing on Schmachtenberger's own real proposed practice of 'yellow teaming' — tracing a proposed action's full network of externalities before building it."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Daniel Schmachtenberger: \"Moving from Naive to Authentic Progress: A Vision for Betterment\""@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct evidence this episode PRECEDES tgs:Interview.TGS_132_WideBoundaryImpactsOfAI chronologically (May 2024 vs. June 2024), confirmed via independent web search: TGS_132's own real show notes directly cite this episode at timestamp 1:45:22 ('A Vision for Betterment | The Great Simplification 126') — a real, confirmed cross-reference, not the reverse. This episode's own 'a year ago... on artificial intelligence and the superorganism' reference is therefore NOT TGS_132 (which postdates this episode) — independent search strongly suggests it's tgs:Interview.TGS_71_ArtificialIntelligenceAndTheSuperorganism (May 2023, real title match, correct ~1-year gap), not yet built in this graph. This also means TGS_132's own existing stub scopeNote — which flagged a 'candidate replay link' (youtu.be/_P8PLHvZygo) as possibly its own — is likely a misattribution: that URL's own real title is 'Artificial Intelligence and The Superorganism,' which matches TGS_71, not TGS_132's real title ('Silicon Dreams and Carbon Nightmares: The Wide Boundary Impacts of AI') — corrected in TGS_132's own scopeNote. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Jaron Lanier, directly cited. This episode's own real page navigation confirms real sequential placement: immediately preceded by tgs:Interview.TGS_125 (Vanessa Andreotti's own real solo TGS episode — already a real, built Human/Persona in this graph, though not yet built as her own Episode individual), and immediately followed by tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_9_TheNextGenerationsDilemma (already fully built in this graph)."@en .

