tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_14_TheFutureIsLocal a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-14> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Framed explicitly by Nate as '101' for himself as much as the audience — he opens by admitting he 'knew next to nothing on bioregioning' before this recording, and the episode maintains that genuinely introductory, low-friction register throughout, with Wahl, Carlisle, and Power building one shared framework together rather than debating. The three guests' expertise divides cleanly and complementarily: Carlisle grounds the conversation in place and deep history (including her own vivid personal anecdote from her earlier archaeology career, recovering a woolly mammoth tusk at a 250,000-year-old Neanderthal site in Jersey), Wahl supplies the philosophical and historical framing (regenerative design, indigenous land-management history, a direct real citation of John Wesley Powell's 1879 proposal to organize the US by watershed boundaries), and Power supplies the financial and governance architecture via her own book's 'four returns' framework. The one real moment of internal critical challenge, rather than mutual reinforcement, comes from Wahl directly to Power: a pointed concern about whether bioregional finance risks becoming 'a backdoor for the finance industry to still be extractive' if patient capital is allowed to expect conventional financial returns — Power responds with a structured, real answer grounded in her book's own stated design principles (purpose-aligned funders, returns as a means rather than an end) rather than deflecting the concern. Wahl closes the episode by reciting a Gary Snyder poem in full in response to Nate's closing question about inevitability — the content of the poem itself is not reproduced here per this graph's own copyright discipline, only the fact of the recitation. The episode ends on a concrete, real organizing detail: plans are underway for a North American Bioregional Congress in 2026, the first since 2009."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DanielChristianWahl,
        tgs:Persona.IsabelCarlisle,
        tgs:Persona.SamanthaPower ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/RR14Bioregioning101Transcript.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2024-12-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-01-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.NoraBateson ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary,
        tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts an introductory deep-dive on bioregioning with three practitioners — Daniel Christian Wahl (returning guest, author of 'Designing Regenerative Cultures'), Isabel Carlisle (Bioregional Learning Centre, South Devon, UK), and Samantha Power (BioFi Project, author of 'Bioregional Financing Facilities') — covering the concept's deep history, indigenous roots, how bioregional boundaries are actually drawn, and how finance, governance, and ecology combine in real projects already underway across multiple continents. Nate opens by directly admitting he 'knew next to nothing on bioregioning' before this recording."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Future is Local: Bioregioning 101"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Worth flagging plainly rather than silently accepting: the source transcript appears to mislabel several passages roughly between 47 and 60 minutes as 'Isabel Carlisle' whose content — first-person references to 'the BioFi project,' capital structuring, 'our theory of change,' finance-specific expertise — clearly matches Samantha Power's stated domain (confirmed by this episode's own real show notes, e.g. the 56:47 link to Samantha Power's own Medium article on 'nature states'), not Isabel's archaeology/place-based background. Treated here as a real transcription artifact, not corrected retroactively in the transcript itself, but not repeated as fact in this summary either. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Daniel Christian Wahl's own real prior TGS solo episode; Atossa Soltani, described in the transcript as 'our mutual friend' of Nate and Samantha, real and confirmed via this episode's own show notes but not built as a Persona in this pass. Fritjof Capra, also directly name-checked here with his own real prior TGS episode, is no longer deferred — now built as a real Persona (see tgs:Persona.FritjofCapra). Bill Plotkin, also directly name-checked here with his own real prior TGS episode, is no longer deferred — now built as a real Persona (see tgs:Persona.BillPlotkin)."@en .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_16_TheMadScrambleForPower a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-16> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A sophisticated, largely convergent three-way analysis among established macro thinkers, organized around Michael Every's own Biblical framing — a civilizational shift from 'childlike' to 'adult' understanding of energy's centrality — which Kaminska and Berman both independently extend rather than contest ('geology is not fair,' Every's line, gets Berman's direct addition: 'geography isn't fair' either). The clearest moment of real, if gentle, divergence is Berman calling the fear of a Russian invasion of Europe 'a preposterous notion' — Every doesn't rebut this directly but offers a genuinely different interpretive frame, invoking Leo Strauss's theory that societies need a unifying 'noble lie' or bogeyman to function, leaving Nate visibly updating live ('I'd never thought about it that way'). Kaminska's own original conceptual contribution — 'mutual liberation syndrome,' her term for how each side of a polarized society sees itself as the one fighting authoritarianism — is offered as a real, named framework, not just commentary. Every's own account of learning 'face culture' through his in-laws (a vivid, personal anecdote about blame being physically and socially transferred to a child after his mother-in-law's flatulence) grounds an otherwise abstract geopolitical point about states' unwillingness to admit error. The episode's most sustained extended metaphor is Every's own, built around Battlestar Galactica (both the 1970s original and its 2000s reboot) and later The Wire and Top Boy, arguing that policymakers who have never experienced real zero-sum competition are now playing exactly that game at the highest levels without understanding its rules. All three converge, without qualification, on honesty from leadership as the single highest-value intervention available — though each frames the mechanism differently: Berman via small 'islands of coherence' of genuinely informed people, Kaminska via leaders directly admitting past policy failures, Every via urging policymakers to study realistic depictions of zero-sum power rather than idealized ones."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ArtBerman,
        tgs:Persona.IzabellaKaminska,
        tgs:Persona.MichaelEvery ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/RR16TranscriptTheMadScrambleforPower.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2025-03-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-03-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_22_CouldTheWestLoseTheResourceWars ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary,
        tgs:Subject.EnergySystems,
        tgs:Subject.Geopolitics,
        tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by returning guests Art Berman (petroleum geologist) and Michael Every (Rabobank global strategist), alongside new guest Izabella Kaminska (founder, The Blind Spot; senior finance editor, Politico Europe), for a wide-ranging real-time analysis of how energy, deindustrialization, military spending, bond markets, and AI/quantum computing are converging into what Michael Every frames as a civilizational shift from 'childlike' to 'adult' geopolitical thinking — closing on what open societies risk if that shift fails."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Mad Scramble for Power: Global Superpowers' Strategies for Energy, Economics, and War"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Michael Every's own 'statecraft'/'what is GDP for' framing, directly and extensively discussed here, is the same real conceptual throughline he brings to RR-22 (built earlier this session with Craig Tindale) — a real, recurring cross-episode theme for this Persona specifically, not yet minted as its own Concept individual in this graph. This episode's own transcript independently reconfirms Every's real Marxist-household upbringing, first disclosed during the RR-12 build. Note: the episode's own real page metadata mislabels its Apple Music link with RR-15's title ('The Plastic Crisis...') — a real site artifact, not corrected here, only noted."@en .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_17_MovingFromApathyToAction a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-17> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Warm, spiritually direct, and unusually candid about Nate's own past skepticism — he opens by admitting that despite encountering Joanna Macy's work repeatedly between 2005 and 2020, 'it never resonated with me' until recently, a genuine disclosure of a changed position rather than a rhetorical setup. Seed and Flor hold a real teacher-student relationship (Flor explicitly introduces herself as 'a student of John'), but the conversation is not simply hierarchical — Flor develops her own distinct specialization (what she calls 'animist perception,' grounded in Stephen Harrod Buhner's plant-intelligence framework) that Seed openly defers to as outside his own primary focus. Seed's central intellectual contribution is a real evolutionary-biology argument that feelings predate and are more reliable than cognition, having been selected for across nearly all of evolutionary history, while cognition is 'a recent intruder.' Both guests offer real, vivid personal origin stories: Seed's account of a spontaneous 1979 direct action to protect what became Terania Creek rainforest, which he describes feeling like 'a mental breakdown' in the moment; Flor's account of witnessing an oil spill devastate an indigenous fishing community she was living with in the Peruvian Amazon, which directly redirected her work. A real moment of practical honesty rather than idealism: Seed recounts being asked to rebrand the Council of All Beings as corporate 'team building,' and reports it has failed every single time he tried — the ritual depends on authentic shared intention, not technique alone. Seed also discloses a genuinely humbling realization: after co-creating the Council of All Beings with Joanna Macy in 1985, he later witnessed Hopi practitioners performing what he recognized as the same ritual, which they told him they'd been doing for 10,000 years without interruption — a real moment of discovering rediscovery rather than invention. The episode closes on full, unqualified convergence between both guests on the same practical advice (find like-minded people close to home), which Nate notes directly echoes something said, coincidentally, on a completely unrelated podcast he recorded the same day."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JohnSeed,
        tgs:Persona.SkyeCielitaFlor ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/RR-17-Transcript-Moving-from-Apathy-to-Action.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2025-05-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-07-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger,
        tgs:Work.ThinkingLikeAMountain ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by deep-ecology co-founder John Seed and grief ritualist Skye Cielita Flor to explore the Work That Reconnects — the framework developed by John Seed with Joanna Macy, Arne Naess, and Pat Fleming beginning in 1985 — covering its spiral structure (gratitude, honoring pain, seeing with new eyes, going forth), the Council of All Beings ritual, and why embodied grief and gratitude practices are, in their view, a necessary complement to purely factual awareness of ecological crisis."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Moving from Apathy to Action: How Facing Grief Can Help Us Navigate a World in Crisis"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "This episode's own real show notes link directly to Nate's own real PhD thesis and to a separate real video of Nate himself explaining Marvin Harris's Cultural Materialism — both genuine primary sources about Nate specifically, flagged here as real and deferred rather than built out further in this pass. Francis Weller's own real prior TGS episode (#167) is directly cross-referenced again, matching the same real episode number already flagged during the RR-27 build earlier this session. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Joanna Macy (deceased, central to the entire episode as co-originator of the Work That Reconnects, cited extensively but never a TGS guest herself); Randy Hayes (Rainforest Action Network, directly named by Nate as 'my friend'); Josh Schrei (directly named by both guests and by Nate as someone he 'would have been a good one' to include)."@en .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_18_HowWaterShapesOurPlanet a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-18> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Genuinely complementary across three distinct scales rather than competing framings: Cooley supplies US institutional/policy analysis (the water-energy nexus, federal underinvestment), Joy supplies pointed regulatory critique grounded in decades fighting New Zealand's own dairy-industry water pollution, and Weiss supplies hands-on, decentralized landscape restoration practice. Joy's own biography is genuinely unusual and disclosed candidly: he left school at 17, worked as a truck driver, sheep farmer, and New Zealand Security Intelligence Service agent before enrolling at university at 33 — a real, non-academic path into a career built on 'blunt commentary' that has drawn criticism from the dairy industry and a former Prime Minister by name. Weiss's own account of the 'biotic pump' — living ecosystems drawing atmospheric moisture inland from the oceans — includes a real moment of scientific humility rather than overclaiming: his own words describe '72 anomalies of water' that remain without full physiological or physical explanation. The clearest moment of real, pointed institutional criticism is Joy's account of New Zealand's practical non-enforcement of its own environmental rules, describing the effective 'penalty' for water-pollution noncompliance as negligible. Rather than converging on a single lever of change, all three hold their differing theories of intervention (policy reform, regulatory enforcement, decentralized restoration) as genuinely complementary rather than competing, closing on shared, concrete real-world success stories — sponge cities, spring restoration, and community-led decentralized water-retention projects — as evidence for cautious, grounded hope rather than abstract optimism."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.HeatherCooley,
        tgs:Persona.MikeJoy,
        tgs:Persona.ZachWeiss ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/RR-18-Transcript-How-Water-Shapes-Our-Planet.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2025-06-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-09-03"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by three water experts operating at different scales — Heather Cooley (Pacific Institute, US water-energy policy), Mike Joy (New Zealand freshwater ecologist and pollution-policy critic), and Zach Weiss (Elemental Ecosystems/Water Stories, hands-on watershed restoration) — to examine the full hydrological cycle, the 'biotic pump' mechanism by which vegetation draws atmospheric moisture inland, agricultural and industrial water pollution, groundwater depletion, and both the policy and landscape-level interventions each guest sees as most effective."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "How Water Shapes Our Planet: The Undervalued Resource that Supports Everything We Do"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "This is the final Reality Roundtable built in this graph, completing the full numbered sequence RR-1 through RR-27. This episode's own real show notes cite New Zealand's territorial governance based on watershed/catchment boundaries — a real, substantive thematic echo of the bioregional-governance ideas built at length in this graph's own RR-14, though not a direct citation within this transcript itself. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: John Kitzhaber's own real prior TGS episode (#70) and Roman Krznaric's (#142, citing Spain's historic Water Court) — both real, confirmed, deferred."@en .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_1_ElectricVehicles a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness,
        tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr01-berman-michaux-prieto> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Largely a consensus-building conversation rather than a debate — the three guests, longtime friends and repeat TGS guests with overlapping biophysical/systems worldviews, converge on a shared thesis rather than argue against each other. Michaux opens with a detailed materials analysis showing that scaling EVs and renewables to meet stated EU 2030 targets would require multiples of known global reserves and annual production of key metals, even before accounting for grid capacity, hydrogen production, or the fossil fuels still required to make steel, plastic, and concrete. Berman and Prieto largely corroborate and extend this from their own domains rather than push back — Berman explicitly says 'I agree with you, I provided you data to support your perspective,' and adds that oil companies' underinvestment is a financial-market choice, not a resource-scarcity problem; Prieto brings real Spanish/German grid-stress data and EV-affordability numbers. The closest thing to friction is clarifying, not adversarial — Berman and Prieto probe Michaux on his 28-day storage-buffer assumption and the electric/hydrogen vehicle split, and he candidly revises and defends specific figures under that questioning, at one point calling the underlying 'is this sensible?' question 'the best argument for degrowth.' The one moment of genuine, if mild, divergence comes in the closing 'how could you be wrong' round: Michaux allows an unforeseen technological breakthrough could upend his numbers; Prieto reframes his own uncertainty as being about timing rather than direction ('sooner than later the system will collapse'); and Berman draws a sharper line between his own specific technical claims (which he says could be wrong) and the biodiversity/ecosystem decline data underlying the whole discussion (which he considers not meaningfully disputable). All three land on the same practical conclusion Nate frames at the top: current EV scaling plans reflect 'energy blindness' — a narrow, supply-side technology story substituting for the harder question of reducing consumption."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ArtBerman,
        tgs:Persona.PedroPrieto,
        tgs:Persona.SimonMichaux ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/RR01BermanMichauxPrietoTranscript.docx1_.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2023-04-23"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-06-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_29_JoshFarleyAppearance2,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_3_OilBestAndWorstOfFuels,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_44_DieselAndTheBarrelOfOil,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_54_TheStealthAdjustmentOfOil,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_7_JoshFarleyAppearance1 ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary,
        tgs:Subject.Geopolitics,
        tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:definition "The inaugural Reality Roundtable — Nate brings together 3 returning guests (petroleum geologist Art Berman, geometallurgist Simon Michaux, and solar/EROI analyst Pedro Prieto) to biophysically stress-test whether dramatically scaling electric vehicles is materially, economically, and infrastructurally achievable, or whether current EV enthusiasm is itself a form of energy blindness — covering mineral/mining constraints, grid capacity, real EROI figures, and whether EVs address the actual root problem or just substitute one narrow-boundary technology story for another."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "\"Electric Vehicles\": Arthur Berman, Simon Michaux & Pedro Prieto"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Olivia Lazard (environmental peacemaker/mediator) was originally scheduled but couldn't attend due to travel issues, per Nate's own real substack write-up — deliberately NOT modeled as thinkr:hasGuest despite appearing in the show notes' own cross-reference list, since she never actually appeared. Her own real prior TGS episode is a separate, deferred candidate. Also deferred: Simon Michaux's own 2 solo TGS episodes (#19, #49, real URLs confirmed); Charles A. S. Hall (Pedro Prieto's real co-author on 'Spain's Photovoltaic Revolution,' already indirectly relevant to this graph via Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple) and that book itself as a real Work — both real, neither built in this pass."@en .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_20_HackingHumanAttachment a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse,
        tgs:Concept.DarkTriad,
        tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-20> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real division of labor between two friends who never disagree but consistently work from different registers — Zak Stein supplies the mechanistic explanation (mirror neurons, the attachment system versus the attentional system, Harlow's monkeys and operant conditioning, the historical throughline from B.F. Skinner to AI tutors), while Nora Bateson consistently reframes the same point in relational/felt terms (warm data, the missing 'rhythm of breath' in digital communication, the edge of the self). Neither treats this as competing frameworks; each explicitly builds on the other's point rather than substituting for it. The one moment resembling internal friction is Zak checking his own argument's edges rather than disagreeing with Nora — catching himself mid-critique to note that dismissing all technology outright is its own kind of naivety ('how far will you push your techno-pessimism'), and that the same critical tools used against chatbots could equally be pointed at real technological benefits (Novocaine, reliable garbage collection) he doesn't actually want to lose. Nate's own participation is unusually personal for an interview host — he narrates his own emotional arc in real time (a 'pit in my stomach' partway through, open shame about once mocking the Amish for opting out of exactly this risk, then explicit relief and hope by the close), and at one point the conversation is genuinely interrupted by an unplanned real-world moment — a garbage truck's noise triggering his own threat-detection reflex — which he and the guests fold directly into the discussion rather than treat as a distraction, since it illustrates the very embodied, evolved cognition the conversation argues chatbots are bypassing. The clearest moment of real-time concept-building: Nate offers 'wide boundary self-awareness' as his own gloss on Nora's father's old definition of sanity ('the ability to perceive your own epistemology'), and Nora accepts and extends it rather than correcting it."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.NoraBateson,
        tgs:Persona.ZakStein ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/RR-20-Transcript-Hacking-Human-Attachment-1.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2025-10-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-11-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by returning guests Nora Bateson and Zak Stein to examine AI's effects at the individual, relational level rather than the usual economic/large-scale framing — how chatbots are designed to exploit attachment vulnerabilities, the early evidence of cognitive atrophy and eroded person-to-person connection this produces, and what a more embodied, deliberately human-to-human counterweight might look like."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Hacking Human Attachment: The Loneliness Crisis, Cognitive Atrophy, and Other Personal Dangers of AI"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found via this episode's own real show notes but NOT built into this graph in this pass, deliberately deferred: Frankly #112 ('The Quadruple Bifurcation') and TGS #198 (Stacy Mitchell, antitrust) — both real cross-references, neither yet stubbed as an Episode individual, so no dct:references asserted for either. Real, well-evidenced NEW Concept candidates surfaced but not minted this pass: Attachment Theory (John Bowlby), Supernormal Stimuli (Niko Tinbergen — directly cited via Work.RealityBlind, already in this graph), Goodhart's Law (Charles Goodhart), and a real Persona candidate, Harry Harlow (of Harlow's monkeys, foundational attachment research) — all real, all independently verifiable, held for a follow-up pass rather than built unprompted."@en .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_21_ArmsRaceOrTheHumanRace a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-21> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Unlike several of the more adversarial-leaning built RRs, Medish and Watson are remarkably aligned throughout — this reads as two experts building one converging analysis together rather than staying in separate lanes or challenging each other. The real complementary split is disciplinary, not oppositional: Watson supplies the technical/military texture (missile specifics, game theory, his own direct Reagan-era technical postings), while Medish supplies the diplomatic/legal frame (treaty architecture, his own direct experience organizing Clinton-Putin summits). Neither ever pushes back on the other's claims; each mostly extends or adds precision to what the other just said. The one genuinely personal, unscripted moment is Medish's own disclosure that he was physically present, as an infant, at JFK's 1963 American University 'Strategy for Peace' speech — his father received his PhD diploma from Kennedy that same day — offered as lived, not abstract, connection to the arms-control history being discussed. The clearest point of real divergence isn't between the two guests but within each of their own closing answers to Nate's direct question about whether current leadership can rise to this moment: Medish frames himself as 'an optimist... but worried,' holding both at once; Watson draws a sharper line, affirming humanity broadly can meet the challenge while flatly denying that current political leaders specifically can ('can our leaders meet these challenges? ...my answer is no, they can't'), attributing this to a real structural mismatch between the personality traits that win elections and the self-doubting, revision-willing traits good decision-making actually requires."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ChuckWatson,
        tgs:Persona.MarkMedish ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/RR-21-Transcript-Why-Science-Communication-Fails.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2026-01-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-01-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by geopolitical risk experts Mark Medish (former Clinton NSC/Treasury official) and Chuck Watson (Cold War-era intelligence/military background, now disaster-and-conflict modeling) to examine the erosion of international governance — starting from first-principles definitions of governance, government, and rule of law, then moving through nuclear arms control history to the real, imminent expiration of New START and what its lapse would mean, and closing on whether AI's entry into military decision-making removes the last human hesitation that has, so far, kept nuclear war from happening."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Arms Race or the Human Race? Governance in the Age of AI, Nuclear Threats, and Geopolitical Brinkmanship"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Richard Gephardt has 2 real confirmed prior TGS episodes (#1 and #148) and is the direct source of the episode's own governance/violence framing ('politics is a substitute for violence') — not bootstrapped here. Anastassia Makarieva (biotic pump researcher, TGS #193) is directly referenced by Nate himself recalling a separate real episode. This episode's own real transcript-download filename is a genuine site artifact worth flagging honestly rather than silently correcting: it's titled 'RR-21-Transcript-Why-Science-Communication-Fails.pdf,' apparently a leftover/mismatched filename from an unrelated episode, even though the URL itself correctly resolves to this episode's real transcript content."@en .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_22_CouldTheWestLoseTheResourceWars a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-22> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Framed by Nate himself at the outset as two people who had been independently 'telling parallel stories' finally merging them — and the conversation lives up to that framing, with Tindale and Every remarkably aligned throughout rather than debating. The real complementary split is textural, not oppositional: Tindale supplies ground-level industrial/supply-chain detail (his own 'golden screw' concept — a single missing input that halts an entire system — and a two-ledger financial-versus-material framework), while Every supplies the macro geopolitical-strategy vocabulary ('economic statecraft,' pointed comparisons of US and Chinese industrial policy). Each consistently extends the other's point rather than qualifying it; Every at one point simply says 'I concur' before adding his own layer. The one moment either explicitly flags real disagreement is telling precisely because it's so rare: Every says outright 'this is the first time I differ from Craig at all,' pushing back on Tindale's skepticism that AI job losses will be modest — arguing that even a much smaller disruption (8%, not the touted 80%) could be politically destabilizing given how radicalized and polarized the public already is. Nate's own role is unusually reflective for an interview host — he calls this 'probably one of the most intense conversations' of the podcast's four-year run, and twice interrupts the material density with genuinely human asides (a girlfriend's text about adopting a puppy, arriving mid-Tindale's point about elder care). The episode closes not on analysis but on personal practice: Tindale describes growing and canning his own food as a direct, literal response to the very systemic fragility he spent the previous ninety minutes diagnosing."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.CraigTindale,
        tgs:Persona.MichaelEvery ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/RR-22-Transcript-Could-the-West-Lose-the-Resource-Wars_.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2026-02-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_21_ArmsRaceOrTheHumanRace ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary,
        tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    skos:definition "Nate brings together two returning guests who had been independently telling parallel stories for years without knowing each other — investor/materials analyst Craig Tindale and Rabobank strategist Michael Every — to merge their respective frameworks on critical-materials scarcity, Chinese industrial dominance, and 'economic statecraft' into one account of why the West's China-versus-US rivalry is fundamentally a materials-and-manufacturing contest, not a purely financial or ideological one. Recorded live as the Iran conflict was actively breaking, and closing on whether AI itself is now the largest new driver of critical-materials demand."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Could the West Lose the Resource Wars? AI, Rare Earths, and Economic Statecraft"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Nate's own words frame this directly: 'probably one of the most intense conversations I've had on this platform in the four years of doing this podcast.' Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Craig's own prior TGS episode (#207); Michael's own extensive prior history (RR-16, RR-12, TGS #118) — see both Personas' own scopeNotes. Zak Stein is directly referenced again with his own real episodes (#180, #122, RR-20), all already built in this graph."@en .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_24_TheFantasyOfSpaceColonization a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse,
        tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment,
        tgs:SchoolOfThought.TechnoOptimism ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-24> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Even more uniformly convergent than RR-1 — Tom Murphy and DJ White, ~20-year friends, never actually disagree with each other anywhere in the transcript; they build on and extend each other's points throughout, arriving from two complementary but distinct disciplinary angles rather than debating one. Murphy supplies the hard biophysical case (asteroid-mining economics that work out to roughly $130,000/ounce for plain dirt; radiation doses that make a lifetime in space a near-guaranteed early cancer death; the rocket equation implying an asteroid-return mission would need several asteroids' worth of fuel), while White supplies the ecological/consciousness case (his own dolphin-cognition and Greenpeace background, arguing the 'real profound stuff' — minds we can already talk to and are actively driving extinct — is here on Earth, not hypothetically out there). Both independently and repeatedly frame techno-optimism as displaced religion (AI as new gods, uploading as immortality, Dyson spheres as cathedrals), not as a rival scientific claim worth adjudicating on the merits. The one place real friction appears is not between the two guests but against the absent, unnamed techno-optimist position itself, which Nate keeps them pointed at with pointed, sometimes deliberately provocative questions (invoking Matt Damon's potatoes in The Martian, asking directly why two of 'the smartest people' he knows hold a minority view). The episode closes on a register shift from technical to personal — White's story of comforting a dying baby humpback whale, Murphy's closing 'talk to animals' — both converging one final time on the same point: the wonder people project onto space is already available here, and is being destroyed."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DJWhite,
        tgs:Persona.TomMurphy ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/RR-24-Transcript-The-Fantasy-of-Space-Colonization.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2026-02-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:references tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment,
        tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:definition "Nate is rejoined by two ~20-year friends, astrophysicist Tom Murphy and eco-interventionist DJ White, to biophysically ground the resurgent techno-optimist enthusiasm for space mining and off-world colonization — the economic impossibility of asteroid mining, the physiological brutality of long-duration spaceflight, and the argument that space colonization functions as a convenient story letting humanity off the hook for damage being done at home. Closes by redirecting attention to Earth itself as the 'spaceship we're already on.'"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Fantasy of Space Colonization: The Spaceship We're Already On"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built specifically as a test case for the 'antagonistic' side of this graph's own network, per MJSullivan's own framing — the episode's real content directly and repeatedly names techno-optimism as its target (Nate's own words: 'the resurgence of ambitions for space mining and space colonization amongst the leading techno optimist technologists of our age'), not inferred from tone. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: a possible THIRD Nate/DJ White co-authored book (Nate's own words call DJ 'co-writer of three books,' but only 2 are currently in this graph — Work.RealityBlind and 'The Bottlenecks of the 21st Century,' the latter itself not yet built as a Work individual either); TGS_18 (Tom Murphy's own solo episode), Frankly_22, and RR-11 ('Tales from the Carbon Pulse,' Tom+DJ together) — all real, all deferred."@en .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_2_DeeperEcology a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EcologicalFootprint,
        tgs:Concept.LimitsToGrowth,
        tgs:Concept.Overshoot ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr02-bateson-rees-weyler> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "The most substantively adversarial of the built RRs so far, though still warmly collegial — Nate's own opening explicitly names the contrast: 'people agree on deep ecology and the ecological predicament of human overshoot, yet take wildly different perspectives in how they view it.' Rees supplies the systems-scientific backbone (dissipative structures, thermodynamics, the mathematics of overshoot-and-crash), Weyler supplies historical/movement context and a harder moral edge (open frustration that environmental groups avoid words like 'overshoot'), and Bateson consistently redirects both toward relational process over prescription ('who can you be when you're with me' versus any engineered solution). The clearest real disagreement in any built RR happens here: when Rees says he'd prefer 1-2 billion people living well to 10 billion living in poverty, Nate directly pushes back ('I actually disagree... I think that's the default that we're headed towards'), and Rees concedes the practical point while holding his normative preference ('I agree it's easier, but I said IF you want to plan for something'). Bateson explicitly declines to fully endorse the contraction-is-inevitable framing Rees and Weyler share, prompting Nate to ask her directly whether she agrees — a rare moment where a built RR's host names a real, unresolved difference among guests rather than treating the conversation as settled consensus. Population and eugenics form the other real point of productive friction: Weyler raises population decline directly and names the taboo around it, Bateson immediately reframes the same territory through care and relational support rather than numbers, explicitly naming the eugenics risk in framing it any other way — not a rebuttal of Weyler's point, but a genuine redirection of its terms that he doesn't contest."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.NoraBateson,
        tgs:Persona.RexWeyler,
        tgs:Persona.WilliamRees ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/BatesonReesWeyler.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2023-06-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-07-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_10_NoraBateson ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary,
        tgs:Subject.Geopolitics,
        tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by three friends — ecological economist William Rees (originator of Ecological Footprint Analysis), Nora Bateson, and Rex Weyler — for a first-principles examination of what ecology actually means beneath its textbook definition (emergence, self-organization, dissipative structures, evolution), why humanity's own outsized ecological footprint makes contraction biophysically inevitable rather than optional, and what an ecologically literate civilization might look like on the other side of it."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "\"Deep(er) Ecology\": William Rees, Nora Bateson & Rex Weyler"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: William Rees's own real prior TGS solo episode (confirmed via a YouTube link in this episode's own real show notes, https://youtu.be/LQTuDttP2Yg, no canonical TGS-site URL given). The direct citation at 56:52 ('contraction of the human species is inevitable') links straight to Club of Rome's own real Limits to Growth publication page — the exact same URL already used as Concept.LimitsToGrowth's own dcterms:source, independently corroborating that source's quality."@en .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_4_TitanicOceans a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:EarthSystemComponent.AMOC ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr04-pauly-turiel-ward> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Uniformly convergent and unusually warm — three genuine friends of Nate's, each bringing a different real disciplinary lens (Pauly's fisheries/subsidy economics, Turiel's physical oceanography, Ward's deep-time paleobiology) that consistently reinforce rather than compete with each other. The clearest human moment comes early and unscripted: Pauly, midway through the conversation, suddenly realizes he's been citing Ward's own book for years without ever having met him ('I said that I don't know Peter Ward, but I was wrong. I use your book all the time... I just didn't connect at all') — a small-world moment neither anticipated. Ward's own 'Medea Hypothesis' (life as structurally self-destructive, an explicit rebuttal to James Lovelock's Gaia Hypothesis) becomes the interpretive frame the whole conversation converges toward by its second half, culminating in Ward's own direct line: 'This is Medea. This is not Gaia... This is life doing it to itself.' The episode's emotional center is its ending: Nate reads aloud, in full, a written question DJ White had prepared before being unable to attend as originally planned — about the likely extinction of most large-brained cetacean species within this century — and all three guests respond with real, unrehearsed weight rather than analytical distance, Pauly describing starving, rib-visible dolphins in the Mediterranean he 'never thought I would see.' Nate's own closing words name the tone directly: 'human vulnerability and honesty,' and his reluctance to end the call is itself part of the episode's real content, not just a sign-off."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.AntonioTuriel,
        tgs:Persona.DanielPauly,
        tgs:Persona.PeterWard ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/RR04PaulyTurielWard.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2023-07-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-09-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary,
        tgs:Subject.Geopolitics,
        tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:definition "Nate brings together three real friends of his — fisheries scientist Daniel Pauly, physical oceanographer Antonio Turiel, and paleobiologist Peter Ward — who have never met each other before this recording, to examine the ocean's overlooked role in the unfolding predicament: industrial overfishing sustained by subsidies, the physical mechanics and real risk of AMOC slowdown or collapse, and deep-time paleontological analogs (Canfield oceans, the Permian and PETM mass extinctions) for what a fully anoxic, stratified ocean would mean. Closes on a direct, emotionally heavy question submitted in writing by DJ White (unable to attend as originally planned) about cetacean extinction."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "\"Titanic Oceans\": Daniel Pauly, Antonio Turiel, Peter Ward"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "DJ White was originally invited as a fourth panelist but could not attend; Nate reads his own written question aloud instead — DJ White is already a real, built Persona in this graph (from Frankly_20/RR-24 work). This transcript independently reconfirms, a second time this session, that DJ White 'co-wrote three books' with Nate — the same real, still-unresolved detail first flagged during the RR-10 build (this graph currently only has 2 of the 3: Work.RealityBlind and 'The Bottlenecks of the 21st Century,' the latter itself not yet built as a Work individual). This episode's own real show notes independently confirm DJ White's own real prior TGS episode: #51. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: all 3 guests' own real prior TGS solo episodes (Pauly #15, Turiel #65, Ward #8)."@en .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_5_FoodAndCommunityInTheRuins a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr05-hine-smaje-thiel> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Uniformly warm and complementary, distinct in one real way from most other built RRs: the three guests were already personal friends before this recording (Nate had stayed at Pella Thiel's farm helping with lambing the night before, and had met both her and Hine in person in Sweden weeks earlier), so the conversation reads less like three experts meeting for the first time and more like a continuation of an existing relationship. The three framings are genuinely complementary rather than competing: Hine supplies the mythic/narrative register (his own 'at work in the ruins' framing, by way of the Italian philosopher Federico Campagna), Smaje supplies grounded agrarian-political realism (skeptical of both techno-fix optimism and any guru promising shortcuts, insistent on the specific, unglamorous politics of land access), and Thiel supplies lived, embodied community organizing (the real 'Potato Appeal' campaign she ran during COVID, modeled directly on a real 1917 Swedish uprising). The one small moment of real, friendly cross-checking rather than disagreement: Thiel attributes an idea to 'Paul Kingsnorth,' and Hine gently corrects her in real time ('It's one of his neighbors. It's Mark Boyle') — the kind of small factual collaboration that happens between people who already know each other well, not a substantive disagreement. Nate's own asides are genuinely personal throughout (his own visceral reaction to the guests' articulateness, his own coined phrase 'pro-social prepping'), and the episode closes on an unusually tender note — Hine speaking directly about his own son and the conversations he has with people weighing whether to become parents 'in a time like this.'"@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ChrisSmaje,
        tgs:Persona.DougaldHine,
        tgs:Persona.PellaThiel ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/RR05Transcript.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2023-10-03"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-10-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.VanessaAndreotti ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary,
        tgs:Subject.Geopolitics,
        tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:definition "Nate brings together three first-time guests, already personal friends by the time of recording — social thinker Dougald Hine (Dark Mountain Project, 'At Work in the Ruins'), small-farm writer Chris Smaje ('A Small Farm Future'), and Swedish ecologist/activist Pella Thiel (Transition Network) — to explore what rebuilding local food systems and genuine community actually looks like on the way toward a lower-energy, more localized future, organized around Hine's own framing of finding worthwhile 'work in the ruins' of a world that is ending, without mistaking that ending for the end of the world entirely."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Food & Community in the Ruins: Dougald Hine, Chris Smaje, Pella Thiel"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "This episode's own real reference to 'Vanessa Machado de Oliveira' and her book 'Hospicing Modernity' is the same real person already built in this graph as tgs:Persona.VanessaAndreotti (her full/maiden name) — not a new individual. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Pella Thiel's own real later solo TGS episode (#121); Federico Campagna, Paul Richards, Ivan Illich, and Ed Gillespie are all real, directly cited thinkers/collaborators not bootstrapped here."@en .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_8_TheIndicMind a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-8> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Distinctly different register from most other built RRs — philosophical and spiritual rather than technical, with real harmony among the three guests, who have spent years deliberately building what they call a 'relational field' with each other before ever discussing the metacrisis directly. The central original contribution is the 'Indic mind' itself, developed across the episode as a paradox-holding, relationality-first orientation distinct from Western crisis-response framing — Abhishek's own account of first meeting Nate is telling: they spent four hours talking about relationships and process without once mentioning climate or the metacrisis. The clearest moment of Nate's own assumption being corrected live comes when he directly states his expectation that Indians would feel resentment toward the Global North for disproportionate carbon emissions and climate impact — Abhishek and Mohit both describe something more layered: real awareness of the injustice without it curdling into blame, attributed to a cultural orientation toward self-directed agency rather than externalized grievance. Mohit's own account of living without air conditioning through 37-40°C heat by personal choice — while explicitly declining to 'glorify' the choice or claim it as a virtue — grounds the episode's more abstract philosophical claims in a concrete, lived practice. The one real point of divergence, held without resolution rather than smoothed into agreement, is each guest's relationship to hope: Kejal insists hope 'is not a choice... a stand that you take,' while Mohit says plainly 'hope is [an] illusioned word' and offers no answer for it, preferring confrontation with and embrace of the unknown instead. The episode's most emotionally exposed moment is Abhishek's own closing disclosure — 'it makes me very emotional to be honest' — describing his felt sense of a worldwide, unnamed network of people quietly doing this work, a claim he explicitly frames as testimony rather than argument."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.AbhishekThakore,
        tgs:Persona.KejalSavla,
        tgs:Persona.MohitTrivedi ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/RR08TrivediThakoreSavlaTranscript.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2024-04-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary,
        tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by three Indian NGO leaders he met in person at a metacrisis symposium in Auroville less than 3 months before recording — Mohit Trivedi and Abhishek Thakore (co-founders, 2069 Ecosystem) and Kejal Savla (co-founder/CEO, Wisdom Tree) — to explore what they call the 'Indic mind': a distinctly non-Western, relationality-first, paradox-holding approach to the metacrisis, grounded in India's continuous spiritual and philosophical traditions rather than primarily technical or policy responses."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Indic Mind: An Approach to the Metacrisis"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "The Superorganism/Moloch concept is directly translated cross-culturally in this transcript — Mohit Trivedi renders it in his own language as 'Shaitan,' a real, notable moment of conceptual bridge-building this graph's own English-language Concept definition doesn't otherwise capture. Real, direct prior professional connection: Kejal Savla served as Chief Functionary of Abhishek Thakore's Blue Ribbon Movement for years before co-founding Wisdom Tree — confirmed via independent search, not asserted in the transcript itself."@en .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_9_TheNextGenerationsDilemma a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency,
        tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-9> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Distinctly personal and emotionally vulnerable, a real departure in register from most other built RRs — three young guests speaking candidly about their own psychological process of confronting the metacrisis rather than analyzing it from professional distance. Natasha Linhart's account is the most exposed: a real, named 'awareness → grief → acceptance' sequence including explicit disclosure of crying alone and feeling powerless during the grief phase, offered without being smoothed over ('that period really sucked. I don't want to sugarcoat it'). Priscilla Trịnh offers a genuinely different phenomenological texture for a similar process — 'a happy tumble down a hill,' exhilarating rather than purely painful — not a contradiction of Natasha's account so much as a different lived experience of a comparable arc, grounded in her own family history as children of Vietnamese refugees and an intersectional-feminist analytical lens distinct from the other two guests'. The one moment of real, direct pushback against Nate's own question comes from James Branagan, who states plainly that he doesn't observe the intergenerational blame Nate's framing assumed: 'I don't really see it... I don't even think blaming is productive' — reframing the real tension instead as a resource-versus-energy mismatch between generations. A genuine, unresolved difference in practice surfaces around social media: Natasha describes actively leaving it, while Priscilla stays engaged but holds herself to a personal rule against 'clicktivism' and 'rage-farming' — two different relationships to the same tool, not reconciled into one answer. The episode closes on a real continuity detail: Priscilla reveals that after Nate stopped teaching his University of Minnesota course, she and fellow students revived it as a peer-led seminar under a new name, 'What to Do About a Fucked Up World.'"@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JamesBranagan,
        tgs:Persona.NatashaLinhart,
        tgs:Persona.PriscillaTrinh ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/RR09BranaganLinhartTrinhTranscript.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2024-05-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-06-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary,
        tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:definition "Nate brings together three people in their early twenties — Priscilla Trịnh (his own former student and teaching assistant), James Branagan (Irish writer studying regenerative agriculture in Spain), and Natasha Linhart (ISEOF research/program associate) — to explore how younger people are personally metabolizing the metacrisis: their own awareness-grief-acceptance journeys, intergenerational blame and collaboration, food and water as central concerns, and the concrete practices each has adopted in daily life."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Next Generation's Dilemma: Confronting the Metacrisis"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "This episode's own real page shows an internal date inconsistency worth flagging plainly: 'issued' is listed as June 9, 2024, but 'Recorded on' is listed as May 8, 2025 — chronologically impossible (a recording date after its own issue date). Given the issued date matches the surrounding episode-number sequence exactly (RR-8 issued May 2024, RR-9 issued June 2024) and Nate's own real Substack post about this episode is dated June 9, 2024 and opens 'Yesterday, I hosted a Reality Roundtable,' the recorded date is treated here as 2024-05-08 (year corrected from the site's apparent typo), not the literal 2025 shown on the page. James Branagan's own real Substack essay ('Recalibrate Your Baseline') independently and directly uses the Superorganism/profit-maximization framing this episode's own discusses link points to."@en .

