    thinkr:hasEconomicXPosition 2 ;
    thinkr:hasEconomicYPosition 2 ;
    thinkr:hasFacet tgs:ScenarioFacet.CascadingBreakdown,
        tgs:ScenarioFacet.ForcedFeudalism,
        tgs:ScenarioFacet.MadMax,
        tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheColdWarScenario ;
    thinkr:hasGeopoliticalXPosition 2 ;
    thinkr:hasGeopoliticalYPosition 2 ;
    thinkr:hasPowerAndDistributionXPosition 4 ;
    thinkr:hasPowerAndDistributionYPosition 2 ;
    dct:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com> ;
    skos:definition "Large-scale human coordination failing across most fronts — local, low-institutional-trust survival, with the complex, just-in-time systems modern life depends on ceasing to function. Explicitly named by Hagens as the darkest of the 4, and framed not as an independent fourth possibility but as the state the other 3 Scenarios slide toward if their own particular sources of stability give out (The Long Repair losing public trust, Mordor Persists hitting an ecological wall it can no longer paper over, Fortress World no longer able to afford its own control apparatus)."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mad Max + Forced Feudalism + Cold War Scenario + Cascading Breakdown"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "RENAMED 2026-08-13 (second rename) from tgs:Scenario.C-H-K-P to a 2-letter mnemonic code, per MJSullivan's explicit request — more readable than an arbitrary single-letter position. prefLabel changed from the bare code to a spelled-out concatenation of its 4 real component Facet names. NOTE, same tradeoff class as this Scenario's own denormalized X/Y positions: this prefLabel is DERIVED FROM (and must stay in sync with) its component Facets' own prefLabels via hasFacet — if a Facet's own name ever changes, this label can silently go stale unless updated to match. Confirmed via grep this rename required updating references in episodes.ttl too (unlike the original TheLongRepair->A-E-I-M rename, which had zero external references at the time)."@en .

tgs:Scenario.MO-CD-GD-QU a thinkr:Scenario,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasAlternateTerm [ a thinkr:AlternateTerm ;
            thinkr:hasAlternateTermType tgs:AlternateTermType.ExactMatch ;
            skos:prefLabel "Mordor Persists"@en ;
            skos:scopeNote "RENAMED 2026-08-13 from tgs:Scenario.MordorPersists to the systematic position-code IRI, per MJSullivan's explicit direction (matching the EnergyReturnOnInvestment precedent — canonical form is the systematic one, human name demoted to AlternateTerm). No dangling references existed before the rename (confirmed via grep across the whole data/seed/ directory)." ] ;
    thinkr:hasEarthSystemsXPosition 4 ;
    thinkr:hasEarthSystemsYPosition 4 ;
    thinkr:hasEconomicXPosition 4 ;
    thinkr:hasEconomicYPosition 2 ;
    thinkr:hasFacet tgs:ScenarioFacet.ACapturedDemocracy,
        tgs:ScenarioFacet.Mordor,
        tgs:ScenarioFacet.QuietUnraveling,
        tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheGeopoliticalDangerZone ;
    thinkr:hasGeopoliticalXPosition 4 ;
    thinkr:hasGeopoliticalYPosition 2 ;
    thinkr:hasPowerAndDistributionXPosition 2 ;
    thinkr:hasPowerAndDistributionYPosition 2 ;
    dct:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com> ;
    skos:definition "Growth that persists on paper while deepening real ecological and social overshoot underneath — roughly a continuation and intensification of the present. Hagens frames it as broadly comfortable for a global minority and genuinely precarious for most of the world, already the lived reality rather than a future scenario for large parts of the Global South. He compares its apparent stability to a cancer's stability within a body — self-perpetuating and outwardly 'healthy' by the metrics used to measure it, right up until the underlying foundation it depends on gives out, at which point collapse is fast rather than gradual."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mordor + Captured Democracy + Geopolitical Danger Zone + Quiet Unraveling"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "RENAMED 2026-08-13 (second rename) from tgs:Scenario.D-G-L-N to a 2-letter mnemonic code, per MJSullivan's explicit request — more readable than an arbitrary single-letter position. prefLabel changed from the bare code to a spelled-out concatenation of its 4 real component Facet names. NOTE, same tradeoff class as this Scenario's own denormalized X/Y positions: this prefLabel is DERIVED FROM (and must stay in sync with) its component Facets' own prefLabels via hasFacet — if a Facet's own name ever changes, this label can silently go stale unless updated to match. Confirmed via grep this rename required updating references in episodes.ttl too (unlike the original TheLongRepair->A-E-I-M rename, which had zero external references at the time)."@en .

tgs:SchoolOfThought.PeakOilMovement a thinkr:SchoolOfThought,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasIntellectualRelationship tgs:Relationship.NateHagens_PeakOilMovement ;
    skos:definition "The loosely-organized, decades-long community of analysts, forecasters, geologists, and commentators studying and publicizing oil depletion dynamics (ASPO, peakoil.com-era forums, etc.) — distinct from tgs:Concept.PeakOil, which is the underlying theory/phenomenon itself. Note on scope: this community is genuinely heterogeneous in rigor and ideology, ranging from serious petroleum-geology analysis to more speculative 'doomer' content, and has no single unifying institution. Included despite that heterogeneity because the core theoretical work (Hubbert Peak Theory) has had multi-decade real-world influence and is a direct formative input to Hagens' own analytical background — he worked professionally in this space before founding The Great Simplification."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Peak Oil (movement/community)"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "NO favorsFacet asserted yet (2026-08-13) — the obvious candidate is the same tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification facet DegrowthMovement now points to (energy contraction forcing exactly this kind of economic trajectory), but asserting that here would be redundant without first finding something genuinely DISTINCT this community argues for — e.g. a specific EarthSystems or Geopolitical-dimension prediction unique to peak-oil analysis (energy-scarcity-driven conflict, resource nationalism) rather than just restating Degrowth's own economic claim from a different angle. Worth revisiting once real source material (an episode, a cited peak-oil-community text) makes that distinct claim explicit."@en .

tgs:SchoolOfThought.PlanetaryBoundaries a thinkr:SchoolOfThought,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.JohanRockstrom ;
    owl:sameAs <http://dbpedia.org/resource/Planetary_boundaries> ;
    skos:definition "Scientific framework (first published 2009, periodically updated since) identifying 9 processes that regulate Earth's stability and resilience, each with a proposed boundary defining a 'safe operating space for humanity' — as of the most recent update, a majority of the 9 are considered transgressed. Led by Johan Rockström, originating at and maintained by the Stockholm Resilience Centre (Rockström's founding institution) in ongoing collaboration with the Potsdam Institute for Climate Impact Research (which he also directs). Directly cited as a foundational influence in this graph's own SchoolOfThought.DoughnutEconomics entry ('ecological ceiling... building on Rockstrom et al.'s planetary boundaries framework')."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Planetary Boundaries"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "NO favorsFacet asserted yet (2026-08-13). Genuine fit question, not just missing evidence: favorsFacet's own semantics are about a worldview arguing a facet is ACHIEVABLE/desirable (like TechnoOptimism/GreenGrowth or Degrowth/GreatSimplification), but Planetary Boundaries is fundamentally DIAGNOSTIC/measurement science, not an advocacy position predicting a societal trajectory — it doesn't argue FOR an EarthSystems outcome so much as measure how far past one we already are. If this ever gets linked, ScenarioFacet.StrainedButWorkable (X=2/Functional, Y=4/ModerateStress) is the plausible target, but only if real source material shows this framework being used to argue that outcome is achievable, not just to measure distance from it."@en .

tgs:SchoolOfThought.Stoicism a thinkr:SchoolOfThought,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    owl:sameAs <http://dbpedia.org/resource/Stoicism> ;
    skos:definition "Ancient Greco-Roman philosophical school. Relevant here specifically for its emphasis on distinguishing what is/isn't within one's control and valuing present right action — the philosophical antecedent cited for the Discount Rates / Present Bias concept."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Stoicism"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "NO favorsFacet asserted (2026-08-13) — the least likely of any SchoolOfThought here to ever get one: this operates at the individual/personal scale (how one person relates to what they can't control), while every ScenarioFacet describes a civilizational-scale trajectory. A genuine category mismatch, not just missing evidence — kept open only because 'never say never,' not because a real path to a link is visible right now."@en .

