thinkr:Series a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "The overarching arc itself (e.g. 'How to Think About the Future'), distinct from any single installment. First instance added 2026-07-11 (Series.HowToThinkAboutTheFuture, with 3 stub Frankly installments) — no longer zero-instance."@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf thinkr:Work,
        dcmitype:Collection ;
    skos:prefLabel "Series"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Dual superclass, same pattern as thinkr:Human (foaf:Person + thinkr:NamedEntity): narrower than thinkr:Work in that it has real Work-like properties (title, host, potentially subject) — but a Series has no standalone content of its own (no replay, no transcript), which is exactly what dcmitype:Collection (an aggregation of resources, membership understood via description of the aggregation) captures and thinkr:Work alone does not. dct:isPartOf/dct:hasPart (used on each installment) are the DCMI-standard properties for exactly this relationship — Collection is their natural target type, not an arbitrary addition."@en .

thinkr:Source a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "A publication or venue (e.g. Wikidata, a specific journal, a specific blog) that one or more thinkr:Evidence individuals cite. Reliability is set ONCE per Source and reused across every Evidence citing it, rather than hand-tuned per assertion."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Source"@en .

tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative a thinkr:Concept,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com> ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.MonetarySystemAndGrowth ;
    skos:definition "The structural requirement of debt-based monetary systems and compound interest for continual economic growth, which Hagens argues locks in ever-increasing energy/material throughput regardless of ecological limits."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Growth Imperative"@en .

tgs:Concept.LimitsToGrowth a thinkr:Concept,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.DennisMeadows,
        tgs:Persona.DonellaMeadows ;
    thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.GayaHerrington ;
    dct:source <https://www.clubofrome.org/publication/the-limits-to-growth/> ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.OvershootAndLimits ;
    skos:definition "Reference to the 1972 Club of Rome-commissioned study modeling interactions between population, industrialization, pollution, food production, and resource depletion, projecting overshoot-and-decline dynamics on multi-decade timescales — a direct intellectual predecessor to Hagens' framework."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Limits to Growth"@en .

tgs:Concept.Overshoot a thinkr:Concept,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:convergesWith tgs:SchoolOfThought.DoughnutEconomics ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.WilliamCatton ;
    dct:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Overshoot_(book)> ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.OvershootAndLimits ;
    skos:definition "The condition of a population/economy consuming renewable and non-renewable resources faster than natural systems can regenerate or absorb waste from them, borrowed from systems ecology (Catton, Meadows) and applied to the global economy."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ecological Overshoot"@en .

tgs:Human.NateHagens a thinkr:Human,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dbo:birthDate "1969"^^xsd:gYear ;
    thinkr:memberOf tgs:AcademicInstitution.UniversityOfChicago,
        tgs:AcademicInstitution.UniversityOfMinnesota,
        tgs:AcademicInstitution.UniversityOfVermont,
        tgs:Organization.PostCarbonInstitute,
        tgs:Organization.TheOilDrum,
        tgs:SchoolOfThought.PeakOilMovement ;
    rdfs:comment "The graph's primary subject — this individual was notably absent until 2026-07-11 despite the entire graph being organized around his framework. NO owl:sameAs: verified 2026-07-11 he has no genuine Wikipedia page (the only hits are EverybodyWiki, not real Wikipedia, and an unrelated Wikidata entry Q51905132 for a different person entirely, 'Nathan A Hagen,' an ORCID-linked researcher with no connection to energy/TGS — confirmed via search, not assumed). Credentials (per the publications/credentials CV scope, not full employment history): PhD in Natural Resources, University of Vermont; Master's in Finance with Honors, University of Chicago Booth School of Business. Pre-TGS career: VP at Salomon Brothers and Lehman Brothers, President of Sanctuary Asset Management, before leaving Wall Street circa 2002-2004. TEACHING, genuine tense conflict between sources as of 2026-07-11 — his own site (natehagens.com/about2) says PAST tense, 'For eight years, Nate taught Reality 101, an honors course at the University of Minnesota'; other sources (RateMyProfessors, Resilience.org) use PRESENT tense. Not resolved either way, flagged honestly rather than guessed. 'Professor' title itself is informal/student usage (RateMyProfessors) — his own bio consistently says 'taught'/'teaches,' never asserts the formal rank. tgs:Work.RealityBlind was the course's primary text (confirmed via a LinkedIn post from Nate himself)."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nate Hagens"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Commonly known as 'Nate' (see skos:prefLabel) — 'Nathan' confirmed as his fuller given name via his own X handle, @NJHagens = Nathan John Hagens. Deliberately not resolving which form foaf:givenName 'should' hold here — flagging the real ambiguity rather than silently picking one, same as the deferred firstName/lastName backlog item. foaf:homepage MOVED to tgs:Persona.NateHagens 2026-07-15 (see that individual's own scopeNote) — a homepage is a public-facing fact like every other Relationship/Episode/Concept-influence fact already migrated under design decision 0f, not biography. It deliberately does NOT include thegreatsimplification.com or its YouTube channel — those arguably belong to TGS THE SHOW as its own entity (not currently modeled at all, same real gap as ISEOF/his employer, also unmodeled despite being confirmed real) rather than to Nate personally. Flagged, not resolved."@en ;
    foaf:familyName "Hagens"@en ;
    foaf:givenName "Nathan"@en .

tgs:Organization.InternationalBatesonInstitute a thinkr:Organization,
