    thinkr:hasProfessionalRelationship tgs:Relationship.JonathanGoldsmith_InternationalBatesonInstitute,
        tgs:Relationship.LucasJackson_InternationalBatesonInstitute,
        tgs:Relationship.NoraBateson_InternationalBatesonInstitute ;
    dct:source <https://www.warmdata.life/international-bateson-institute> ;
    skos:definition "A nonprofit foundation for transcontextual research in ecology, economy, social change, health, education, and art — founded and led by Nora Bateson (president), registered in Sweden (sources vary on whether also US-registered). Institutional home for Bateson's 'Warm Data' methodology and Warm Data Lab certification program; Jonathan Goldsmith and Lucas Jackson are both affiliated as core team member and certified host, respectively."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "International Bateson Institute"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "CORRECTED 2026-08-13, caught before this pass was finished: first draft mistakenly used owl:sameAs for this org's own homepage — this project's established convention reserves owl:sameAs specifically for DBpedia/Wikidata equivalence (confirmed via the Organization.MaEarth precedent: 'NO owl:sameAs... no genuine Wikipedia page found'), not general external links. No Wikipedia/DBpedia page found for this org — dcterms:source used instead for its own homepage."@en .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_13_FutureCouncil a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-13> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely distinct episode in tone and format — Nate first interviews adult director Damon Gameau briefly before three young activists join. Gameau's own central intellectual contribution is his 'Three Hats' framework: that people (including the corporate leaders he filmed) are simultaneously economic actors, individuals seeking a good life, and aspiring good ancestors, and that current economic structures make it nearly impossible to hold all three at once — a real, substantive explanation for why even caring executives feel trapped. He discloses candidly that the filmmaking process personally 'cracked him open' emotionally, his most profound experience as a director. The three young guests offer real, differentiated (not uniform) assessments of their actual corporate meetings: they describe Decathlon's response as genuinely responsive (one of their suggestions — multiple name-tag spaces on clothing for reuse — was reportedly put into practice), in direct contrast to Nestlé and ING, which they describe as evasive when pressed on fossil-fuel-to-renewable transition plans. Their own economic reasoning is notably sophisticated for their age: a direct 'drug dealer' analogy (framing Exxon and Shell as suppliers who shift blame onto addicted consumers) and a separate 'cookie' analogy illustrating how blame gets redirected from the powerful onto the vulnerable. Skye Neville discloses a real, unresolved personal tension rather than claiming false consistency: extensive international travel for the film's promotion directly conflicts with her own environmental values, which she addresses through concrete but self-acknowledged imperfect offsetting (50 consecutive beach cleanups, tree planting) rather than denial of the contradiction. Nate's own closing philosophical framing — describing modern flight as 'indistinguishable from magic,' powered by dense ancient energy most people take entirely for granted — is offered as a direct extension of the conversation rather than a scripted transition. The episode closes with practical, peer-to-peer advice from the children themselves on managing climate anxiety: taking small concrete actions, allowing themselves to grieve openly rather than suppressing distress, and resisting the pressure to personally solve the crisis alone."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ClemenceCurrie,
        tgs:Persona.DamonGameau,
        tgs:Persona.JoaquinMinana,
        tgs:Persona.SkyeNeville ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://youtu.be/gi5VXVc2N6c> ;
    dct:created "2024-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-11-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.FutureCouncilFilm ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary,
        tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:definition "Nate first briefly interviews documentarian Damon Gameau, then is joined by three of the eight young activists (ages 11-14) featured in Gameau's film 'Future Council' — Skye Neville (North Wales), Clemence 'CC' Currie (Scotland), and Joaquin Minana (Netherlands) — about their real experiences confronting corporate leaders (Nestlé, Decathlon, ING) directly about climate accountability, and their own reflections on 'Groth,' the film's animated antagonist character explicitly built on Nate's own real Economic Superorganism concept."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Future Council: How Children are Responding to our Planetary Crises"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "This is the only episode in this graph sourced from a cleaned-up YouTube auto-generated transcript rather than an official TGS-hosted PDF (the site hosts no downloadable transcript for this episode) — treated with somewhat more caution regarding exact wording, though the substance is clear. This episode's own real show notes independently confirm the direct link between the film's 'Groth' character and Nate's own real Economic Superorganism paper. Three of the eight children featured in the film appear as guests; the other five are not covered in this pass. Personal-life details incidental to the interview (unrelated to the guests' environmental advocacy work) are omitted from this summary per this graph's general practice of scoping content to what is substantively and publicly relevant."@en .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_15_ThePlasticCrisis a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-15> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Deeply collegial and values-aligned throughout — Nate himself names the real dissonance directly: 'off camera... we were laughing and joking... and it's fricking disgusting.' The episode opens with a real intellectual move worth distinguishing from simple agreement: asked whether they agree with Jeremy Grantham's claim (made in a separate real TGS episode) that plastics/EDCs pose a bigger threat than climate change, Birnbaum partially concurs, but Dixon and Trasande both immediately reject the framing itself — arguing plastics and climate are inseparable, not competing threats, since plastics are fossil fuels and their production directly undermines any 1.5°C pathway. That reframing, not the original yes/no question, becomes the episode's real throughline. Nate's own CPAP mask is used as a recurring, genuinely personal anchor for his anxiety about untestable everyday plastic exposure. The clearest moment of real, extended speculation is Trasande's direct comparison to The Handmaid's Tale, predicting future conflict not simply over food and water but specifically over safe, uncontaminated food and water — Nate responds candidly that he'd 'never thought of that before,' a real live reaction rather than a rehearsed follow-up. Dixon deliberately pivots the conversation's tone using the Montreal Protocol as a real model of policy success, naming the shift herself ('I felt like we did go a bit doom mongering there for a second'). The episode's most vivid first-hand account is Trasande's own description of being the last public speaker at the Busan plastics-treaty negotiations at 2:30am, with Dixon revealing she stayed in the room specifically to support him. Nate's closing reframe of Dixon's self-deprecating 'I wish I was rich' comment — 'you are already rich, given what you're doing with your life' — is a small, values-driven correction offered warmly rather than argued."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ChristinaDixon,
        tgs:Persona.LeoTrasande,
        tgs:Persona.LindaBirnbaum ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/RR15TranscriptPlasticCrisis.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2025-01-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-02-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_23_ThePlasticDetox,
        tgs:Persona.ShannaSwan,
        tgs:Persona.SianSutherland ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary,
        tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by three plastics researchers/advocates — pediatrician Leo Trasande (NYU), toxicologist Linda Birnbaum (former NIEHS/NTP director), and campaigner Christina Dixon (Environmental Investigation Agency, UN Global Plastics Treaty negotiations) — for a detailed examination of plastic's human-health impacts (endocrine disruption, cardiovascular disease, fertility, cancer), the regulatory 'innocent until proven guilty' status quo, the failure of recycling as a solution, and the state of the ongoing UN plastics treaty process, including a first-hand account of the Busan negotiations."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Plastic Crisis: A Health and Environmental Emergency"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "This episode was already anticipated as a real, deferred reference during this graph's own RR-23 build ('Reality Roundtable #15, The Plastic Crisis') — that link is now real and bidirectional. This episode's own real show notes explicitly flag a factual correction: 'Nate misspoke this statistic in the episode' regarding hourly global plastic-waste figures — the transcript's spoken numbers (5.7 million toothpaste tubes, 570,000 cell phones, 2.3 million pairs of shoes) are corrected by the source itself to roughly 20 billion toothpaste tubes, 20 billion shoes, and 5 billion cell phones yearly; the error is preserved here as fact-about-the-recording, not repeated as a true statistic. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Jane Muncke, Martin Scheringer, and Jeremy Grantham are all named directly in this episode's intro as prior TGS plastics guests, with real confirmed episode links, but are not yet built as Persona individuals in this graph — a prior draft of this graph incorrectly claimed Grantham already existed here, corrected now. Pete Myers is directly named by Birnbaum as a real personal connection to Nate ('my friend Pete Meyers') — not built as a Persona in this pass."@en .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_3_UnlearningEconomics a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment,
        tgs:SchoolOfThought.DoughnutEconomics,
        tgs:SchoolOfThought.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr03-erickson-farley-raworth-keen> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "The most substantively adversarial-yet-collegial of the built RRs — the only one where two different guests explicitly announce they're playing 'devil's advocate' against a co-panelist's argument, not just adding texture. Raworth does it twice: first pressing Erickson on what actually replaces the rational-actor model once you've torn it down ('then what?'), and later challenging Farley's rationing proposal directly, arguing markets plus redistribution and a universal basic income would work better than rationing given rationing's own real historical failure modes (black markets, Soviet-style central planning). Farley doesn't concede — he counters with a specific alternative (guaranteed access to essential goods specifically, not a general UBI, because essential-good prices are too volatile for a fixed income to track), and the two continue trading specifics rather than one yielding. Keen plays devil's advocate 'in a different direction,' agreeing entirely with Erickson's critique of neoclassical economics but pushing further — arguing the theory fails on its own internal mathematical terms even before you get to its empirical failures. The clearest real disagreement, though, isn't among the guests but in how each answers Nate's own closing question — can any of this actually be taught at scale? Keen is bluntly pessimistic ('I don't think they can'), Farley says he 'sadly agrees a little bit' but still points to concrete, working programs, and Raworth explicitly frames herself as 'leaning into possibility,' citing a real curriculum win (the Doughnut now appearing in an International Baccalaureate economics textbook) as evidence change is already happening, however slowly. All four converge without disagreement on Raworth's own closing 'Embedded Economy' diagram — Erickson calls it 'not rocket science' as a genuine compliment, and Raworth explicitly welcomes that as the whole point."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JonErickson,
        tgs:Persona.JoshFarley,
        tgs:Persona.KateRaworth,
        tgs:Persona.SteveKeen ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/RR03EricksonFarleyRaworthKeenTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2023-07-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary,
        tgs:Subject.Geopolitics,
        tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:definition "Nate brings together four heterodox economists — two of them his own real PhD advisors, Jon Erickson and Josh Farley — each given roughly 6 minutes to name one core assumption of mainstream Econ 101 they consider fundamentally broken: Erickson on Homo economicus and the rational-actor model, Keen on energy's near-total absence from macroeconomic theory, Farley on the assumption that markets efficiently allocate essential resources, and Raworth on GDP-centric growth as the discipline's implicit endpoint. The stated goal is blunt: roughly 235 million university students worldwide are being taught a model of the economy that doesn't track physical reality, and the episode is explicitly framed as something to show in classrooms."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "\"Unlearning Economics\": Jon Erickson, Josh Farley, Steve Keen, and Kate Raworth"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: all 4 guests' own real prior TGS solo episodes are now confirmed with real URLs (Erickson #45, Keen #30, both via RR-3's own real show notes; Raworth's via YouTube, no canonical TGS-site URL). Real, specific named academic references that could become their own citation-only Personas if this thread is revisited: Elinor Ostrom (Nobel laureate, commons governance), Thorstein Veblen, E.O. Wilson (Consilience) — none built here."@en .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_6_FossilFreeFoodSystems a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr06-bradford-millison-shiva-zetah> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Highly convergent — Jason Bradford's own mid-episode summary names it directly: 'I think we're all kind of singing the same song here.' The one moment of genuine, explicit reframing rather than agreement comes from Daniel Zetah, who opens his turn by directly challenging Nate's own question: 'I would actually like to just challenge the question itself' — arguing the premise that the current fossil-fueled system is even 'feeding the world' well is itself false, given documented malnutrition and obesity coexisting under it. A second, milder real disagreement is one of degree rather than direction: Bradford's opening statement calls for depopulating cities outright, while Millison pushes back gently that 'not all cities are created equal,' pointing to cities historically sited on rivers and trade routes as potentially viable in a lower-energy future rather than uniformly abandoned. Shiva grounds the whole conversation in real, specific longitudinal data from her own farm (20 years of soil nitrogen, zinc, and manganese measurements comparing organic and chemical-input plots), giving the discussion an empirical anchor the other three build on rather than dispute. The episode's most self-aware moment is Bradford's own 'Shire' analogy via Lord of the Rings, which he flags himself as 'corny' before continuing anyway — Nate affectionately teases him for it at the close ('I knew you were corny') while praising his 'agro-ecological wisdom.' All four converge fully, without qualification, on ending agricultural subsidies and patents on seed/life as the necessary first policy steps — the clearest full consensus point in the episode."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.AndrewMillison,
        tgs:Persona.DanielZetah,
        tgs:Persona.JasonBradford,
        tgs:Persona.VandanaShiva ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/RR06BradfordMillisonShivaZetahTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2023-10-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-11-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary,
        tgs:Subject.Geopolitics,
        tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:definition "Nate reunites four returning guests — farmer/biologist Jason Bradford (Post Carbon Institute), permaculture educator Andrew Millison (Oregon State University), physicist-turned-agroecology-activist Vandana Shiva (Navdanya), and Minnesota regenerative farmer Daniel Zetah — around one central question: could 8+ billion people be fed with minimal fossil fuel inputs, and what would that actually require in labor, land, culture, and policy? The conversation moves from de-urbanization and village-scale redesign through Shiva's real longitudinal soil-nutrition data to closing visions of each guest's own bioregion in 2050."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fossil Free Food Systems: Jason Bradford, Andrew Millison, Vandana Shiva, Daniel Zetah"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "William Rees is directly cross-referenced by Bradford ('a friend of yours, Nate, and mine') via the exact same real prior-episode YouTube URL already used for tgs:Persona.WilliamRees during this session's RR-2 build (https://youtu.be/LQTuDttP2Yg) — independent confirmation, not a new fact. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: real later solo TGS appearances for both Bradford (2026, Farming Clubs) and Millison (TGS #178, 2025); Bradford's real prior episode #76 is shared with Zetah's own prior appearance (a joint episode, not two separate ones — worth flagging as a real, unusual case of two guests sharing one numbered prior episode, though not verified further in this pass)."@en .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_7_PovertyBlind a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr07-hoopes-kilde-perry-sulamoyo> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Highly collaborative among four practitioners who mostly extend rather than contest each other's points, anchored throughout by Hoopes's own ALICE data (41% of US households below a survival budget despite employment). The clearest moment of real, pointed directness rather than diplomatic framing comes from Marc Perry, describing the frustration in Black communities at having raised the same structural concerns for decades to what he calls 'deaf ears' — 'it took somebody white to say it in order for you to actually pay attention to it.' A second genuinely candid moment, flagged by the speaker himself as sensitive, is Dalitso Sulamoyo raising real community tension over resource allocation to recent asylum seekers versus longtime residents — he calls it 'quite a touchy issue' and does not soften it further. Peter Kilde grounds the episode's abstract inequality argument in specific documented history — a detailed comparison of Hurricane Katrina and Hurricane Maria's radically unequal disaster responses, including real death tolls and recovery-fund disparities — building on his own extensive, real personal connection to New Orleans and a professional history with peak-oil and resource-depletion work (ASPO-USA board membership) that predates and directly parallels Nate's own subject matter. Marc Perry's own personal disclosure — growing up materially poor but 'rich' in family and community, not realizing he was poor until told — offers a lived counterpoint to the episode's data-heavy first half. The two guests' closing interpretive frames diverge memorably without conflicting: Kilde insists poverty is fundamentally 'a math problem,' not a behavioral one, while Perry frames the culture's complacency through Huxley's Brave New World rather than Orwell's 1984 — people appeased into indifference rather than terrorized into it."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DalitsoSulamoyo,
        tgs:Persona.MarcPerry,
        tgs:Persona.PeterKilde,
        tgs:Persona.StephanieHoopes ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/RR07HoopesKildePerryandSulamoyoTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2023-11-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-12-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary,
        tgs:Subject.Geopolitics,
        tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:definition "Nate convenes four anti-poverty practitioners — Stephanie Hoopes (creator of the ALICE financial-hardship measure), Peter Kilde (West CAP, Wisconsin, and Nate's real-life neighbor with a working relationship dating to 2010), Marc Perry (Community Action Inc. of Rock and Walworth Counties), and Dalitso Sulamoyo (Champaign County Regional Planning Commission, Illinois) — to examine US poverty beyond the federal poverty line, using Hoopes's ALICE metric to show 41% of US households cannot afford a bare-minimum survival budget despite being employed, often in multiple jobs. The conversation moves through disaster-response inequality (Katrina, Maria), racial disparity, and closes on what community-level resilience can do as resource constraints from the Great Simplification deepen."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Stephanie Hoopes, Peter Kilde, Marc Perry, Dalitso Sulamoyo: \"Poverty Blind\""@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Paul Ehrlich (real TGS #9, not yet built as a Persona) and Dennis Meadows (real TGS #12 — Meadows already exists as a real, built Persona in this graph, cited here only in passing, not as a guest, so his own record was not touched in this pass)."@en .

