tgs:ScenarioDimension.EarthSystems a thinkr:ScenarioDimension,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:xAxisHighLabel "Unraveling Biosphere"@en ;
    thinkr:xAxisLowLabel "Functional Biosphere"@en ;
    thinkr:yAxisHighLabel "Moderate Climate Stress"@en ;
    thinkr:yAxisLowLabel "High Climate Stress"@en ;
    skos:definition "The state of the biosphere/climate. Per Part 4, Hagens treats this Dimension as structurally different from the other 3: humanity isn't starting from a neutral baseline (real ecological damage is already locked in), and severe Earth-system stress constrains what's achievable on every other Dimension rather than being just one more independent variable."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Earth Systems"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Axis labels confirmed directly from MJSullivan's own original screenshot of this framework's visual grid (Image 2, shared at the very start of this whole project) — the highest-confidence of the 4 Dimensions' axis assignments, sourced from the primary visual itself rather than inferred from prose."@en .

tgs:ScenarioDimension.Economic a thinkr:ScenarioDimension,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:xAxisHighLabel "Overshooting Ecological Limits"@en ;
    thinkr:xAxisLowLabel "Toward Ecological Limits"@en ;
    thinkr:yAxisHighLabel "Global Economy Growing"@en ;
    thinkr:yAxisLowLabel "Global Economy Contracting"@en ;
    skos:definition "Whether the economy is growing or contracting, and whether that growth/contraction stays within ecological limits or continues in overshoot. Per Part 4 (Frankly #148), the same direction of economic change can play out very differently depending on the other 3 Dimensions — this axis alone doesn't determine what daily life actually feels like."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Economic"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "CORRECTED 2026-08-13 — MJSullivan supplied a real, complete matrix PDF derived directly from the 'How to Think About the Future' series ('TGS: How to Think About the Future'), confirming this Dimension's axis assignment had been backwards since first built: Growth/Contraction is the real Y-axis and Ecological-Limits/Overshoot is the real X-axis, not the reverse as previously stored. The underlying facts were never wrong (each Facet's own real-world meaning was correct), only which axis-letter carried which real dimension — now corrected to match the primary source's own labeling exactly, including its exact wording ('Global Economy Growing/Contracting', 'Toward/Overshooting Ecological Limits')."@en .

tgs:ScenarioDimension.Geopolitical a thinkr:ScenarioDimension,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:xAxisHighLabel "Self-Sufficiency"@en ;
    thinkr:xAxisLowLabel "Interdependence"@en ;
    thinkr:yAxisHighLabel "Cooperative Relations"@en ;
    thinkr:yAxisLowLabel "Adversarial Relations"@en ;
    skos:definition "The degree of cooperation vs. hostility/fragmentation between nations and regions."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Geopolitical"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "CORRECTED 2026-08-13 — MJSullivan's real matrix PDF (see ScenarioDimension.Economic's own scopeNote for the source) revealed the X-axis low/high labels were inverted: real low=Interdependence, high=Self-Sufficiency, not the reverse as previously stored (Y-axis was already correct). The (Adversarial, Interdependence) quadrant's Facet individual, tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheGeopoliticalDangerZone, only needed a position fix — its name was briefly, incorrectly changed to 'A Captured Democracy' based on a cut-and-paste error in MJSullivan's first supplied PDF, then restored once a corrected PDF confirmed the original name was right all along. See that Facet's own scopeNote for the full, honest back-and-forth."@en .

tgs:ScenarioDimension.PowerAndDistribution a thinkr:ScenarioDimension,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:xAxisHighLabel "Concentrated Structure"@en ;
    thinkr:xAxisLowLabel "Distributed Structure"@en ;
    thinkr:yAxisHighLabel "Broad Gains"@en ;
    thinkr:yAxisLowLabel "Narrow Gains"@en ;
    skos:definition "Who holds power and how material gains/surplus get shared. Per Part 4, Hagens treats this as the single biggest determinant of lived experience — two Scenarios can have near-identical material/energy conditions and still be radically different places to live, based on this Dimension alone."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Power and Distribution"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "LEAST CONFIDENT of the 4 Dimensions' axis assignments — Frankly_139's own page describes this Dimension only loosely ('how concentrated each is and where the gains flow'), never as cleanly as Economic/Geopolitical/EarthSystems. 3 of 4 Facets (CivicIdeal, ACapturedDemocracy, ForcedFeudalism) are used by an already-named Scenario and cross-checked against their own definitions; TheStewardshipDeal's position is assigned by elimination only, with zero real source material to verify against — flagged, not asserted with full confidence."@en .

tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheGlobalizationIdeal a thinkr:ScenarioFacet,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:diagonallyDisjointWith tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheColdWarScenario ;
    thinkr:hasFacetCode "GI" ;
    thinkr:hasQuadrantNode tgs:QuadrantNode.15 ;
    thinkr:hasScenarioDimension tgs:ScenarioDimension.Geopolitical ;
    thinkr:hasXPosition 1 ;
    thinkr:hasYPosition 5 ;
    thinkr:xAxisDivergentFrom tgs:ScenarioFacet.FriendlyNeighborsWithGoodFences ;
    thinkr:yAxisDivergentFrom tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheGeopoliticalDangerZone ;
    skos:definition "Cooperative and interdependent geopolitics — trade flows freely, shared institutions manage disputes. Hagens' own real characterization: broadly what the world aimed at from 1990 to roughly 2015, extremely efficient, but 'everything is wired to everything else,' so a disruption anywhere eventually shows up everywhere else."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Globalization Ideal"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Position CORRECTED 2026-08-13 from (X=4,Y=4) to (X=2,Y=4) — see tgs:ScenarioDimension.Geopolitical's own scopeNote for the full correction (real chart PDF supplied by MJSullivan; the X-axis low/high labels were inverted). Real-world meaning unchanged (Cooperative + Interdependence); only the axis scale was wrong. Definition ADDED 2026-08-13, same day, from Frankly_139's own full real transcript, resolving the gap noted when this Facet was first built."@en .

tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheStewardshipDeal a thinkr:ScenarioFacet,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:diagonallyDisjointWith tgs:ScenarioFacet.ACapturedDemocracy ;
    thinkr:hasFacetCode "SD" ;
    thinkr:hasQuadrantNode tgs:QuadrantNode.55 ;
    thinkr:hasScenarioDimension tgs:ScenarioDimension.PowerAndDistribution ;
    thinkr:hasXPosition 5 ;
    thinkr:hasYPosition 5 ;
    thinkr:xAxisDivergentFrom tgs:ScenarioFacet.CivicIdeal ;
    thinkr:yAxisDivergentFrom tgs:ScenarioFacet.ForcedFeudalism ;
    skos:definition "Concentrated political/economic power combined with broadly shared material gains — a small group holds real authority, but outcomes reach most of the population because those in power choose to, or need to, keep the population functioning. Hagens' own real examples: Singapore and East Asian post-war development states. Explicitly flagged by Hagens as stable but fragile over time, since it depends entirely on the continued competence and goodwill of whoever holds power."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Stewardship Deal"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Definition ADDED 2026-08-13 from Frankly_139's own full real transcript, resolving a gap flagged since this Facet was first built ('not defined in any of the 6 transcripts reviewed' referred to an earlier, smaller transcript set)."@en .

tgs:SchoolOfThought.TechnoOptimism a thinkr:SchoolOfThought,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:favorsFacet tgs:ScenarioFacet.GreenGrowth ;
    thinkr:hasAlternateTerm [ a thinkr:AlternateTerm ;
            thinkr:hasAlternateTermType tgs:AlternateTermType.BroadMatch ;
            skos:prefLabel "Hopium"@en ;
            skos:scopeNote "Raised by MJSullivan, citing a Reddit r/ANRime discussion (site blocked from direct fetch here; the post text was pasted directly instead). That primary source reveals real, meaningful register variation this note should reflect rather than flatten: in that fandom community's own internal usage, 'hopium' denotes hope grounded in some real (if inconclusive) evidence — explicitly contrasted with 'copium,' described there as genuinely baseless self-delusion ('lying to yourself'), with 'doomium' as hopium's opposite and 'reach' covering strained, unlikely theories. This is a softer, more neutral sense than the general internet-slang usage independently confirmed via Collins Dictionary, Merriam-Webster's slang entry, and crypto-culture sources (CoinGecko, etc.), which frame 'hopium' as itself unrealistic or baseless — the dominant sense in broader/tech-adjacent discourse, and the one this graph's own connection to techno-optimism actually rests on (critics invoking 'hopium' against techno-optimist claims are typically using this harsher, more common sense, not the community-specific neutral one). BroadMatch remains the right type either way: both senses are broader than techno-optimism specifically, one domain-specific instance among many the term can apply to."@en ] ;
    dct:source <https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s13347-022-00555-x> ;
    skos:definition "The belief that technological progress and human ingenuity can resolve ecological and resource constraints without requiring a fundamental reduction in material consumption — spanning positions from general faith in innovation to explicit projects like space mining and off-world colonization. A direct, named target of critique across multiple TGS episodes (RR-24's own official description centers the whole episode on it: 'Techno-optimists... dream of a future where we mine, travel to, and colonize other planets... all in the hopes of bypassing the problems we now face on Earth'), and thematically opposed by Concept.EnergyBlindness throughout this graph's own already-built material."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Techno-optimism"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "favorsFacet added 2026-08-13, linking into the 256-Scenario matrix per MJSullivan's explicit request. GreenGrowth (Economic dimension, X=4/Growth Y=4/WithinLimits) is the precise structural match for techno-optimism's own core promise: continued growth, decoupled from resource constraints via technology. The tension this surfaces is real and worth keeping visible: GreenGrowth's own scopeNote already records that Hagens himself considers it 'the least realistic option on this Dimension,' calling continued material growth and biosphere healing 'two forces that pull against each other too hard to hold together at a global scale.' This property doesn't resolve that disagreement — it makes it queryable: the SchoolOfThought that argues FOR this facet, and the Concept's own author's independent assessment that it's the least likely one, now sit on the same graph, connected through the same facet, rather than as two disconnected prose observations."@en .

tgs:SchoolOfThought.WarmData a thinkr:SchoolOfThought,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:source <https://www.warmdata.life/international-bateson-institute> ;
    skos:definition "A methodology developed by Nora Bateson (~2012 onward), defined as transcontextual information about the interrelationships that integrate a complex living system — contrasted with 'cold' data that isolates variables from their relational context. Operationalized through the 'Warm Data Lab' workshop format, which the International Bateson Institute has since certified over 600 hosts to run internationally."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Warm Data"@en .

