tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DarkTriad,
        tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-19> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Complementary rather than adversarial, but distinct in texture from the other RRs — Nancy McWilliams and Reid Meloy defer to and build on each other throughout rather than debate, yet each brings a genuinely different disciplinary lens rather than illustrative texture around a shared thesis. McWilliams opens by explicitly distancing her own approach from trait-based diagnosis ('I don't tend to think in terms of traits, I tend to think in terms of themes'), then supplies detailed clinical material Meloy doesn't have — how she actually works with psychopathic patients in therapy, her critique of the DSM's own diagnostic history. Meloy supplies forensic and evolutionary material she doesn't have — population prevalence estimates, predator/prey framing, extreme case data from his own casework. The one moment of honest gap rather than synthesis: when Nate raises Scott Barry Kaufman's 'Light Triad,' Meloy admits he isn't familiar with the term at all, and McWilliams is the one who has looked it up and explains it. Where the two genuinely converge, they amplify rather than merely agree — when McWilliams calls the entire history of the United States 'somewhat psychopathic,' Meloy doesn't qualify it, he extends it ('the theme of psychopathy has been woven through the history of the United States'). Nate's own contribution goes beyond interviewing: he draws a real cross-disciplinary connection between his own energy/power background and the psychological concept of power ('power in an ecology sense is an ecosystem... accessing an energy gradient per unit time'), and at the close, builds Meloy's closing metaphor (Dark Triad individuals as 'passengers on the train' who must never be allowed to drive) directly into his own recurring Superorganism framing, explicitly flagging he needs to think further about how the two connect rather than presenting it as settled."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.NancyMcWilliams,
        tgs:Persona.ReidMeloy ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/RR-19-Transcript-Dark-Triad-Personality-Traits.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2025-07-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-09-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by psychoanalyst Dr. Nancy McWilliams and forensic psychologist Dr. Reid Meloy to examine the Dark Triad (psychopathy, narcissism, Machiavellianism) and Dark Tetrad (adding sadism) from complementary clinical and evolutionary angles — why these traits persist in the population despite their costs, how they manifest differently in individual relationships versus positions of power, and what real, non-naive guardrails (strong local community, truth-telling, functioning democratic institutions) might do to contain their worst effects at a civilizational scale."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dark Triad Personality Traits: How Psychopathy, Narcissism, and Machiavellianism Impact Our Cultures & Social Systems"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "2026-08-13: fully rebuilt from a complete real transcript + real fetched show notes (MJSullivan's own upload, cross-verified against a direct fetch of the episode's own page), replacing the earlier bare stub built from RR-26's own cross-reference alone. Real, further citable material found but NOT built into this graph in this pass: David Sloan Wilson (real, own TGS episode #56, confirmed via this episode's own real show notes) — a genuine further multi-level-selection/Superorganism connection, deliberately deferred rather than chased down unprompted. 'TGS Episodes 1 + 2' for Zak Stein in this episode's own show notes are ordinal labels for his 1st/2nd appearances (real URLs confirm #122/#180), not literal episode numbers — resolved via direct link inspection, not assumed."@en .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_23_ThePlasticDetox a thinkr:Episode,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse,
        tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-23> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Warm and deeply collegial throughout — Swan and Sutherland are established real-world colleagues (co-present at UN Plastics Treaty negotiations) as well as friends, and Nate's own opening explicitly names the shift from 'meeting you two as activists and scientists' to being actual friends after four-plus years. The complementary split is expertise-based, not oppositional: Swan supplies the clinical/epidemiological data (urinary metabolites, sperm counts, the documentary's own specific intervention protocol), while Sutherland supplies industry and policy strategy (the 'cattle prod and lightning rod' framing for A Plastic Planet's work, the Plastic Free Babies campaign). One moment stands out as genuinely different from every other RR built this session: asked directly for the documentary's central result — how many of the six couples became pregnant — Swan deliberately declines to answer, wanting viewers to watch the film rather than get a spoiler, and Sutherland backs her ('no spoilers from Shanna'). This isn't evasion in the sense of avoiding a hard question; it's a real, distinct interactional dynamic — a promotional and narrative consideration overriding an otherwise-answered pattern of direct scientific replies throughout the rest of the episode. The other genuinely honest moment comes when Nate raises a deliberately uncomfortable question — whether awareness of these interventions could become an 'evolutionary filter' where only the informed and wealthy can afford to reduce their exposure and successfully reproduce — and Swan responds with real epistemic humility rather than a rehearsed answer: 'I think it sounds brilliant and I don't have any response.' The closest thing to a substantive correction comes from Nate himself at the close, gently but directly qualifying Sutherland's framing that 'we' collectively care about the environment: 'We do care about the environment. The system doesn't care' — offered as an addition to her point rather than a rebuttal, but a real distinction he clearly wanted on the record."@en ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ShannaSwan,
        tgs:Persona.SianSutherland ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/RR-23-Transcript-The-Plastic-Detox.pdf> ;
    dct:created "2026-02-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_15_ThePlasticCrisis ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:definition "Nate reunites two returning guests and real-world colleagues — reproductive epidemiologist Shanna Swan and plastics-industry campaigner Sian Sutherland — around Swan's new Netflix documentary 'The Plastic Detox,' in which she runs a real 3-month endocrine-disrupting-chemical-reduction intervention with 6 couples facing unexplained infertility, tracking urinary chemical metabolites and sperm quality before and after simple household product swaps. The conversation moves from the documentary's specific findings to the broader case that plastics and endocrine disruption are the 'micro' version of the same fossil-fuel-driven story as climate change, and closes on Sutherland's Plastic Free Babies campaign targeting the first 1,000 days of life."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Plastic Detox: Reducing Endocrine Disruptors for Better Fertility and Human Health"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Shanna Swan's own real first-ever TGS appearance (#2, one of the podcast's earliest episodes) is no longer deferred — now built as a real Episode individual (tgs:Interview.TGS_2_ShannaSwan), with a real dct:references link back to this episode. Sian Sutherland's (#89) real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: solo episodes with Jane Muncke (#154, #104), Wes Carter (#186), Jeremy Grantham (#155, #99), and Martin Scheringer (#37, PFAS) — none built as Persona or Episode individuals in this pass; a prior version of this note incorrectly claimed Grantham was already built here, which was never actually verified and is corrected now. Reality Roundtable #15 ('The Plastic Crisis') is no longer deferred — now built as a real Episode individual, with a real dct:references link back to this episode."@en .

